// Runtime/GtRuntime.maxon — the emitted GREEN-THREAD scheduler runtime (Workstream R3, slice P1.5-B1a).
//
// ⭐ SINGLE-M COOPERATIVE. One OS thread runs everything; a `.data` global holds the currently-running
// GT and a single global FIFO run queue holds the ready ones. There are NO worker threads, NO
// work-stealing, NO per-P sharding, NO atomics and NO TLS — every one of those is a LATER slice (B1b
// multi-M). Porting v1's GMP scheduler here would be porting its cost curve (and its lock/TLS
// machinery) into a runtime that has exactly one thread; v1 is DEBUGGED, not FAST, and none of that
// machinery has a consumer at this rung. So this is v1's KNOWLEDGE — the GT/P struct offsets, the
// context-switch frame shape, the await/complete protocol — with v1's multi-M apparatus left out.
//
// THE SPLIT (see PLAN.md's R-box + how shv2 already ships `mrt_start` alongside MmRuntime):
//   HAND-ASSEMBLED raw bytes (X64GtRuntime.maxon) — `__gt_context_switch` and `__gt_trampoline` ONLY.
//     rsp/rbp manipulation, pushing/popping physical callee-saved registers, and `ret`-into-a-fresh-frame
//     are unrepresentable in Std, so they are bytes. Neither touches a `.data` global (see below).
//   BUILDER-BUILT `StdModule` (here) — `__gt_init`/`__gt_spawn`/`__gt_enqueue`/`__gt_dequeue`/`__gt_await`.
//     Ordinary loads, stores, arithmetic, compares, branches and calls, so they get register allocation,
//     the frame layout and the leak-safe backend for free — exactly MmRuntime's reasoning one tier up.
//
// ⭐ WHY THE HAND CHUNKS NEED NO `.data`. `__gt_current_gt` is managed ENTIRELY by the builder-built
// drivers: `__gt_init` seeds it to GT0 (the adopted main thread), and `__gt_await` sets it to the GT it
// switches INTO and back to itself on resume — the only two places a switch is driven. The context
// switch passes the `to` GT through in rdx (which it never modifies), so `__gt_trampoline` reads its own
// GT from rdx rather than from a global; and the trampoline's completion switch always targets a waiter
// the driver set, so it needs no fallback global either. That keeps both hand chunks pure register/stack
// code — the same shape as the `mrt_*` panic runtime — with no `.data` relocation machinery to add.
//
// ⚠ SCALAR ONLY (B1a). An async call's arguments and its awaited result are integer/bool (GPR) values;
// the parser REJECTS a managed (String/struct) or float async arg/result (E2015) rather than leak or
// miscompile — the trampoline moves the result through R8 and the args through the GPR arg registers, so
// a float (XMM) or a managed pointer would need a channel B1a does not build. Managed async is B1c.
//
// ⭐ RELOCATING MORESTACK (P1.5-B1a′ slice 2). Each GT gets a tiny `GtStackSize` (2 KB) `osAllocPages` seed
// stack; every guarded function's prologue projects its frame against `gt.stackGuard` (X64GtRuntime's
// `emitGtStackGuard`) and, when it would overflow, calls the hand-assembled `__gt_morestack` to allocate a 2x
// stack, COPY the old one onto it, walk the saved-rbp chain fixing every interior pointer by the relocation
// offset, and continue on the larger stack. So deep recursion inside an `async` body just works; the seed is a
// floor, not a ceiling. The GT struct + stack + arg buffer are `__slab_alloc`'d / `osAllocPages`'d — INVISIBLE
// to the `__mm_alloc_count` leak gate — so an un-awaited `async f()` (which never runs) leaks only its 2 KB
// seed (OPEN #88), and an awaited GT's grown stack is RELEASED on completion (`__gt_drive_until` → `osFreePages`).

// --- GreenThread struct offsets ---
// A SMALLER struct than the authoritative 0xD8 GtLayout (which carries the multi-M / fault-diagnostic
// fields B1a does not build), but the offsets it DOES use are byte-identical to that layout
// (Arm64MacosGreenThread.maxon:83-92), so the B1a′/B1b ports graft on without moving a field.
// The offsets the hand-assembled chunks (X64GtRuntime — the context switch, the trampoline, and now the
// syscall shim) read/write are EXPORTED so the byte code and this builder name one struct layout — the
// offsets are a contract the two sides share, not a fact each restates. `GtOffStackBase` is exported
// because the shim's GT-stack guard reads it (`stackBase == 0` ⇒ run the kernel call directly, no switch);
// `GtOffStackSize`/`GtOffNext`/`GtOffIoHandle` are written only from Std here, so they stay local.
export let GtOffSp = 0x00  // saved stack pointer at the context-switch point
export let GtOffFp = 0x08  // saved frame pointer (rbp) at the context-switch point
export let GtOffStatus = 0x10  // GtStatus* below
export let GtOffStackBase = 0x18  // low address of the osAllocPages'd stack (0 for GT0, which uses the OS stack)

// EXPORTED because the relocating morestack (P1.5-B1a′, X64GtRuntime) reads the CURRENT stack size to compute
// the doubled size and the memcpy count, and writes the new size back here after growing.
export let GtOffStackSize = 0x20
export let GtOffResult = 0x28  // the R8 return value, captured by the trampoline when the GT completes
export let GtOffWaiter = 0x30  // the GT to context-switch back to when this GT completes (set by the driver)
let GtOffNext = 0x38  // run-queue FIFO chain
export let GtOffFuncPtr = 0x40  // the async function's address
export let GtOffArgBuf = 0x48  // the __slab_alloc'd argument buffer (MaxAsyncArgs i64 slots)

// The green-thread stack GUARD (P1.5-B1a′): the low-water address a function's PROLOGUE compares its projected
// rsp against — `projectedRsp < stackGuard` means the frame would overflow, so the prologue calls `__gt_morestack`
// to grow+relocate the stack first. `__gt_spawn` seeds it to `stackBase + GtStackGuardMargin`; `__gt_morestack`
// updates it to `newBase + GtStackGuardMargin` after a grow; GT0 keeps the slab-zero (0), so its guard-compare
// (`projectedRsp >= 0` unsigned) always passes — GT0 runs on the real OS stack and never grows. EXPORTED because
// the hand-assembled prologue guard (X64GtRuntime) reads it and `__gt_morestack` writes it. Offset 0x50 is the FREE
// word between `argBuf@0x48` and `threw@0x58`, so it fills the reserved gap without moving any field.
export let GtOffStackGuard = 0x50

// The dual-register ERROR FLAG the trampoline captures from R10 when a THROWING async thunk returns
// (P1.5-B2b): 0 = success, non-zero = threw (a scalar error's `ordinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias`, or a boxed
// union's box pointer — whatever the thunk left in R10). Read ONLY by `__gt_try_await`, which returns it as
// the second register of its dual-register exit; `__gt_await` (a non-throwing await) never reads it, so a
// non-throwing thunk leaving garbage in R10 here is harmless. Offset 0x58 (a one-word gap at 0x50) is
// byte-identical to the authoritative GtLayout (`Arm64MacosGreenThread.maxon:93`), so a later port grafts on.
export let GtOffThrew = 0x58

// The child process HANDLE the running GT is parked on (P1.5 subprocess seed). Written by `__gt_proc_add` when a
// GT parks on `runProcess`, read back after the GT resumes to reap the exit code and close the handle. Offset
// 0x80 is exactly the `io_handle` slot the authoritative GtLayout names in the 0x60–0xA0 reserved gap (see the
// note below), so the later IOCP/subprocess-pipe slices graft on without moving a field. Written and read only
// from Std here, so — like `GtOffStackBase`/`GtOffNext` — it stays local, not exported to the hand-asm chunks.
let GtOffIoHandle = 0x80

// The GT's OWN Windows TIB stack bounds, saved so the syscall shim (P1.5-B1a′) can repoint `gs:[0x08]`
// (NT_TIB.StackBase) / `gs:[0x10]` (NT_TIB.StackLimit) at the system stack for a kernel call and RESTORE
// them to this GT's stack afterwards — heavy kernel calls probe the TIB and fault when RSP is outside its
// bounds. `__gt_spawn` seeds them to the GT's own stack (`tibStackBase = stackBase + GtStackSize`,
// `tibStackLimit = stackBase`); GT0 keeps 0/0 from the slab-zero (the shim never runs for GT0 — its
// `stackBase == 0`). EXPORTED because the shim (a hand-assembled Target-tier chunk) reads them. Offsets
// 0x90/0x98 are byte-identical to the authoritative GtLayout's `tib_stack_base`/`tib_stack_limit`
// (`GtLayout.cs:82-83` / `Arm64MacosGreenThread.maxon`), so the B1b multi-M port grafts on without moving a
// field; they fill two words of the 0x60–0xA0 reserved gap this rung otherwise leaves zeroed.
export let GtOffTibStackBase = 0x90
export let GtOffTibStackLimit = 0x98

// The PARKED flag (P1.5 mid-body-yield). Set by `__gt_sleep` just before a SPAWNED GT switches back to its
// driver ("I am off my run queue, suspended in the timer heap"); cleared on resume. Offset 0xA8 is the SAME
// slot the authoritative GtLayout (`Arm64MacosGreenThread.maxon` / v1 `GtLayout`) calls `io_yielded`, so the
// later subprocess/IOCP slices — whose in-flight-I/O fields (`io_result_val@0x60`, `io_handle@0x80`, …) fill
// the 0x60–0xA0 gap this rung leaves ZEROED-BUT-UNUSED — graft on without moving a field. Its role at THIS
// rung is documentation + the resume contract; single-M has no race, so it is a plain flag, not the
// multi-M park-gate the reference uses it for.
export let GtOffIoYielded = 0xA8

// ⭐ THE INLINE ARGUMENT REGION (P1.5-B1c #87). MaxAsyncArgs i64 slots living at the END of the GT struct,
// so a spawn needs NO separate `__slab_alloc`'d argument buffer — `__gt_spawn` sets `gt.argBuf` (0x48) to a
// SELF-POINTER `gt + GtOffArgInline`, and the trampoline dereferences that self-pointer exactly as it read a
// separate buffer (X64GtRuntime:155 is byte-identical). This DIVERGES from both references — v1
// (`Arm64MacosGreenThread.maxon:2613-2616`) and the bootstrap (`ARM64CodeEmitter.Runtime.cs:4188-4194`) keep
// the arg buffer a separate `__slab_alloc` and `__slab_free` it at run-completion — because shv2's slab has NO
// free path (`MmRuntime.__slab_alloc` is a pure bump allocator). Inlining reclaims the arg region for free when
// the struct is recycled onto the free-list, and it is SAFE because the region is DEAD by completion: the
// trampoline reads all MaxAsyncArgs slots into registers at run-start and never touches them again. Offset 0xB0
// is the current end of the reserved header, so every existing field (0x00–0xA8) stays byte-identical to the
// authoritative GtLayout and the port property is preserved.
export let GtOffArgInline = 0xB0

// 0xE0 = 224 bytes = 0xB0 header + ArgBufBytes (0x30) inline arg region. The header words 0x60–0xA0 remain
// RESERVED (the reference's io_result/io_handle/cancel/tib/trace fields), slab-zeroed and unused until the
// subprocess/IOCP slice fills them. Must be a multiple of 8 (whole-word fields).
export let GtStructSize = GtOffArgInline + ArgBufBytes

// --- GT status ---
// `ready` (the slab-zeroed initial state), `completed` (set by the trampoline, read by the drive loop's
// recycle gate) and `waiting` (set by `__gt_sleep` while parked on a timer, cleared to `ready` by
// `__gt_timer_check` when the deadline fires) are load-bearing. `running` (1) exists in the reference layout
// but is unused here — nothing reads it in single-M. `waiting` MUST be distinct from `completed` so the drive
// loop's `status == completed` recycle gate NEVER recycles a merely-parked GT's stack (the #1 mid-body-yield
// trap: recycling a yielded GT's stack is a use-after-free when it resumes).
let GtStatusReady = 0
export let GtStatusCompleted = 2
let GtStatusWaiting = 3

// The green-thread stack: the INITIAL region a spawned GT gets. The relocating morestack (P1.5-B1a′) grows it
// on demand (2x per guard fault, copy + rbp-chain relocation), so this is a small SEED, not a ceiling — the
// syscall shim (slice 1) already moved every kernel call off this stack onto the 64 KB system stack, which is
// what makes a tiny GT stack safe. ⚠ BRING-UP: kept at 0x100000 (1 MiB) while the guard is proven inert, then
// shrunk to 0x800 (2 KB) once morestack is disassembly-verified — see this file's git history / the B1a′ brief.
export let GtStackSize = 0x800

// The GUARD MARGIN (P1.5-B1a′): the cushion a function's prologue projects below its frame before comparing
// against the stack guard, and the offset `__gt_spawn`/`__gt_morestack` place the guard above the stack base. It
// appears TWICE in the effective trigger (`projectedRsp = rsp - frameBytes - GtStackGuardMargin`, guard =
// `stackBase + GtStackGuardMargin`), so morestack fires while `rsp - frameBytes` is still `2*GtStackGuardMargin`
// above the true overflow point — the slack that covers the guarded function's pushes plus any UN-guarded runtime
// (`__`/`mrt_`) frames that free-ride on the caller's margin between guard checkpoints. 0x3A0 (928 B) is v1's
// value (`X64Backend.maxon:7293`), carried because the same frames it was sized for run here.
export let GtStackGuardMargin = 0x3A0

// The single global SYSTEM (scratch) stack a green thread's kernel calls run on (P1.5-B1a′ slice 1): a 64 KB
// region allocated once in `__gt_init`, its TOP published to `__gt_system_stack_top`. The syscall shim
// switches RSP here for a Win32 call so the kernel's deep stack usage (RPC, TIB probes) never touches the
// GT's own small stack — the infrastructure that makes slice 2's 2 KB GT stacks safe. 0x10000 is byte-
// identical to the authoritative layout's `PSystemStackSize` (`GtLayout.cs:178`). EXPORTED because the shim
// subtracts it from the system-stack top to compute the TIB StackLimit it publishes for the call.
export let GtSystemStackSize = 0x10000

// The saved-register frame `__gt_context_switch` builds on a GT stack: the five callee-saved GPRs
// (rbx, r12, r13, r14, r15 — shv2's callee-saved set, `RegisterAllocator.calleeSavedMask`, spelled once as
// `calleeSavedOrder` in X64PrologueEpilogue) plus the return-address slot = 6 words = 48 bytes. `__gt_spawn`
// lays a fresh such frame whose TOP word is `&__gt_trampoline`, so the first switch-in's final `ret` enters
// the trampoline. It MUST byte-match the context switch's push/pop order — a mismatch is a wild `ret` — so the
// Target tier's `assertGtFrameConsistent` (X64GtRuntime) CHECKS this constant against `calleeSavedOrder.count()`
// at chunk-build time; if a callee-saved register is added there without moving this number, an async compile
// panics rather than miscompiling.
export let GtInitialFrameBytes = 48  // 5 callee-saved words + the return-address word (checked in X64GtRuntime)

// The argument buffer holds MaxAsyncArgs i64 slots. The trampoline loads ALL of them into the GPR
// argument registers unconditionally (a callee reads only the slots it declares; the rest are the
// slab's zeros in unread registers), so there is no arg COUNT to carry and the trampoline needs no
// count-guarded loads. MaxAsyncArgs equals the parser's parameter cap.
export let MaxAsyncArgs = 6

// One argument buffer slot per argument, i64-wide. The stride the lowering STORES at, the trampoline LOADS
// at, and `ArgBufBytes` sizes by — one fact, so the three cannot drift.
export let GtArgSlotBytes = 8
export let ArgBufBytes = MaxAsyncArgs * GtArgSlotBytes

// --- Timer store (P1.5 mid-body-yield) ---
// The parked-GT deadline store: a fixed array of (deadline, gt) entries, one per sleeping GT. Its ENTRY
// LAYOUT mirrors the reference exactly — `TimerEntrySize` bytes, `TimerOffDeadline`@0, `TimerOffGt`@8, capacity
// `TimerHeapCapacity` — so a min-heap discipline can be retrofitted later with zero layout change. At THIS
// rung it is an UNSORTED array (append to add; linear scan to find the earliest / fire the due ones), a
// DELIBERATE simplification over the reference's min-heap: the concurrent-timer count is bounded by the number
// of simultaneously-sleeping GTs (2 in the interleave spec, 1 in a sequential sleep loop), where O(n) is
// indistinguishable from O(log n), and a hand-built-Std sift-down is exactly the subtle-corruption class this
// substrate must avoid. The heap region itself is `__slab_alloc`'d once in `__gt_init` (a 4 KiB zeroed block)
// rather than a `.data` reservation, because `.data` entries are single scalar slots.
let TimerHeapCapacity = 256
let TimerEntrySize = 16
let TimerOffDeadline = 0
let TimerOffGt = 8
let TimerHeapBytes = TimerHeapCapacity * TimerEntrySize

// The empty-store sentinel `__gt_timer_earliest` returns: a real deadline is `now_ns() + ms*1e6`, a huge
// positive nanosecond instant, so 0 unambiguously means "no timers".
let TimerNoDeadline = 0

// --- Process store (P1.5 subprocess seed) ---
// The parked-child store: TWO PARALLEL arrays, `procHandles[i]` (the child's process HANDLE) and `procGts[i]`
// (the GT waiting on it), each an i64 slot. A DELIBERATE departure from the timer store's INTERLEAVED (deadline,
// gt) layout: `WaitForMultipleObjects` wants a CONTIGUOUS handle array, so keeping handles in their own array
// lets `procHandles` feed the netpoll's wait DIRECTLY with no gather copy and no gather buffer — the access
// pattern genuinely differs from the timer store's (which reads deadline+gt together in its earliest-scan), so
// the layout follows it. Both regions are `__slab_alloc`'d once in `__gt_init` (like the timer heap), because
// `.data` entries are single scalar slots. Removal is swap-with-last across BOTH arrays in lockstep.
//
// ⚠ CAPACITY IS `MAXIMUM_WAIT_OBJECTS` = 64, and it is a HARD, ENFORCED bound — not a comment. `WaitForMultipleObjects`
// refuses an `nCount` above 64, so at most 64 children may be parked SIMULTANEOUSLY. `__gt_proc_add` CHECKS
// `count >= ProcStoreCapacity` before every append and aborts (`RuntimeAbort.processStoreOverflow`) rather than writing
// `procHandles[64]`/`procGts[64]` past the 64-slot slab arrays — an unchecked append would be heap corruption, and
// then `emitProcWait` would pass `pc > 64` to `WaitForMultipleObjects`, which fails → the same null-handle busy-spin
// class. The subprocess-seed specs park ≤3 at once (three concurrent children; one process + one sleeper in the
// interleave case), far below the cap, and the harness uses ~12 workers; real >64-way fan-in is the later IOCP rung
// (C), which has no consumer yet. A documented, SAFE hard bound — never silent corruption.
let ProcStoreCapacity = 64
let ProcSlotBytes = 8  // one i64 handle / gt pointer per parallel-array entry
let ProcStoreBytes = ProcStoreCapacity * ProcSlotBytes

// --- Win32 wait / process constants (P1.5 subprocess seed) ---
// `WAIT_OBJECT_0` (0) is what `WaitForSingleObject`/`WaitForMultipleObjects` return for a SIGNALED handle (an
// exited child); `__gt_proc_check` polls each handle with a zero timeout and tests for it.
let WaitObject0 = 0
let WaitPollTimeout = 0  // WaitForSingleObject timeout=0 — poll, never block (the netpoll owns the blocking wait)
let WaitInfinite = 0xFFFFFFFF  // INFINITE — the netpoll's block when no timer bounds the wait

// STARTUPINFOA is 0x68 bytes; its first field `cb` must equal that size. Every other field is 0 (slab-zeroed),
// so `dwFlags = 0` and the child inherits the parent console — `cmd /c exit N` runs and exits with its code.
let StartupInfoSize = 0x68
let StartupInfoCbOffset = 0

// PROCESS_INFORMATION is 0x18 bytes: hProcess@0, hThread@8 (dwProcessId@16, dwThreadId@20 are unused here).
let ProcInfoSize = 0x18
let ProcInfoHProcessOffset = 0
let ProcInfoHThreadOffset = 8

// The 8-byte slab slot `GetExitCodeProcess` writes the child's DWORD exit code into; the runtime reads it back
// as a zero-extended u32 (a 4-byte read that ignores the high dword, so a dirty high half cannot corrupt it).
let ExitCodeSlotBytes = 8
let TrailingNulBytes = 1  // the one extra byte for the cmdline's NUL terminator (slab-zeroed → free)

// ⭐ **THE TWO ABORT CODES THIS FILE DIVERGES WITH ARE `RuntimeAbort`'s, NOT ITS OWN**, and this file is half
// the reason that registry exists: its `ProcStoreOverflowExitCode` and `ArrayRuntime`'s element-size-zero code
// were both 70, each picked against a list that could not see the other. The numbers now live in one enum whose
// duplicate check is the language's (E3031), so "distinct from every other abort" is proven rather than argued.
//
//   * `RuntimeAbort.subprocessSpawnFailed` — `__gt_process_run` when `CreateProcessA` FAILS (a command whose
//     first token is not a runnable executable). On failure CreateProcessA leaves the slab-zeroed
//     PROCESS_INFORMATION untouched, so `hProcess == 0` detects it — and the runtime MUST NOT park on a null
//     handle, which would busy-spin the netpoll forever (WaitForMultipleObjects on a NULL handle returns
//     WAIT_FAILED immediately, so the poll never fires). A hard `ExitProcess`-style abort is the honest fatal
//     response (a message-carrying panic would need the parser's `.rdata` interner, which the builder-built
//     runtime has no handle to at this rung — a richer diagnostic is a follow-up). `runProcess` on a valid
//     command never reaches here.
//   * `RuntimeAbort.processStoreOverflow` — `__gt_proc_add` when the process store is FULL (a program parks
//     more than `ProcStoreCapacity` = 64 children concurrently, beyond `WaitForMultipleObjects`'s
//     `MAXIMUM_WAIT_OBJECTS`). It is distinct from every subprocess-spec child exit code
//     (1/2/3/4/5/25/45/123), so the store-overflow regression test pins exactly this path. Aborting is the
//     honest SAFE response to hitting the seed's documented concurrency ceiling; real >64-way fan-in is the
//     later IOCP rung.

// --- Monotonic clock (P1.5 mid-body-yield) ---
// `ns = ticks * 1e9 / freq`, evaluated as the OVERFLOW-SAFE split identity
// `ns = (ticks/freq)*1e9 + ((ticks mod freq)*1e9)/freq` — see `buildGtNowNs`.
let NanosPerSecond = 1000000000
let NanosPerMilli = 1000000

// --- Runtime function names ---
export let GtContextSwitchName = b"__gt_context_switch"
export let GtTrampolineName = b"__gt_trampoline"

// The relocating stack-grower (P1.5-B1a′). A hand-assembled chunk (X64GtRuntime), NOT a builder-built Std
// function — it manipulates rsp and walks the saved-rbp chain, unrepresentable in Std. EXPORTED because the
// prologue guard's `call __gt_morestack` (X64Backend) and the chunk's own name must agree — one spelling.
export let GtMorestackName = b"__gt_morestack"
export let GtInitName = b"__gt_init"
export let GtSpawnName = b"__gt_spawn"
export let GtEnqueueName = b"__gt_enqueue"
export let GtDequeueName = b"__gt_dequeue"
export let GtAwaitName = b"__gt_await"
export let GtTryAwaitName = b"__gt_try_await"

// P1.5 mid-body-yield entry points. `__gt_sleep` is the front-end target for `sleep(ms)`; the rest are the
// scheduler internals it and the await drivers share.
export let GtSleepName = b"__gt_sleep"
let GtDriveUntilName = b"__gt_drive_until"
let GtNowNsName = b"__gt_now_ns"
let GtTimerAddName = b"__gt_timer_add"
let GtTimerCheckName = b"__gt_timer_check"
let GtTimerEarliestName = b"__gt_timer_earliest"

// P1.5 subprocess seed. `__gt_process_run` is the front-end target for `runProcess(cmd)`; the rest are the
// scheduler internals it and the netpoll share.
export let GtProcessRunName = b"__gt_process_run"
let GtProcAddName = b"__gt_proc_add"
let GtProcCountName = b"__gt_proc_count"
let GtProcCheckName = b"__gt_proc_check"

// --- `.data` globals (all i64, zero-initialized) ---
// The zero image IS the initial state: no current GT, an empty run queue, init not yet run — exactly as
// MmRuntime's zeroed slab cursor means "no arena yet". So there is nothing to run before `main`.
// EXPORTED because the syscall shim (X64GtRuntime, a Target-tier hand-assembled chunk) loads the running GT
// from it to gate the stack switch and to restore the TIB after the call. One label, named once, shared by
// the Std drivers that own the slot and the shim that reads it.
export let GtCurrentGtLabel = b"__gt_current_gt"  // the running GT; drivers own it (see the header)
let GtRunQueueHeadLabel = b"__gt_run_queue_head"
let GtRunQueueTailLabel = b"__gt_run_queue_tail"
let GtInitDoneLabel = b"__gt_init_done"  // idempotency guard for __gt_init

// The TOP of the single global 64 KB system (scratch) stack, published by `__gt_init` (`base +
// GtSystemStackSize`). EXPORTED because the syscall shim reads it to switch RSP for a kernel call. A FIXED
// top, reset every entry (NOT a bump pointer): the shim and slice 2's morestack both switch to it, safe only
// because they never nest (kernel calls are leaf and single-M runs one thing at a time — see the shim's note).
export let GtSystemStackTopLabel = b"__gt_system_stack_top"

// ⭐ THE FIXED-SIZE STACK FREE-LIST IS GONE (P1.5-B1a′). B1a recycled a completed GT's stack onto a LIFO because
// every stack was a uniform 1 MiB; the morestack makes stacks VARIABLE-SIZED (2 KB, doubled on demand), so a
// fixed-size free-list would hand a 2 KB-believing `__gt_spawn` an 8 KB grown stack (or vice versa) — a size the
// spawner records as `GtStackSize` while the reservation is something else, which the guard then mis-projects
// against and morestack mis-copies. So the STACK lifecycle is ALLOC-FRESH-ON-SPAWN + FREE-ON-COMPLETE: `__gt_spawn`
// commits a fresh `GtStackSize` seed, `__gt_morestack` releases the OLD stack when it grows, and `__gt_drive_until`
// releases the completed GT's (possibly grown) stack via `osFreePages` — so a spawn/await loop's STACKS stay bounded
// by alloc+free churn rather than a recycle. (A same-size 2 KB free-list for stack reuse is a pure optimization left
// to a later pass.)
//
// ⭐ THE GT STRUCT free-list (P1.5-B1c #87). The struct — unlike the variable-sized stack — is a FIXED GtStructSize,
// so a free-list is size-safe: `__gt_drive_until` PUSHES a completed struct onto `__gt_free_list_head` (a `.data`
// LIFO, linked through the now-idle `GtOffNext` run-queue slot) and `__gt_spawn` POPS one before falling back to a
// fresh `__slab_alloc` — so a spawn/await loop reuses ONE struct rather than bump-leaking one per iteration (the slab
// has no free path). This is v1's `next`-linked LIFO recycle (`Arm64MacosGreenThread.maxon:2364-2410`/`:2987-3005`)
// MINUS its per-P sharding (single-M ⇒ ONE global head) and its 64-cap-then-`__slab_free` overflow (shv2 has no
// `__slab_free` ⇒ an uncapped free-list that never frees is the natural fit). The never-awaited GT still leaks its
// seed stack (OPEN #88 — a never-run GT never completes, so it never reaches the recycle path); this rung closes
// only the awaited-GT struct+arg-buffer leak (#87).
let GtFreeListHeadLabel = b"__gt_free_list_head"

// ⭐ THE COMPLETION-BASED LIVE-STRUCT COUNTER (P1.5-B1c #87) — what makes the struct leak a GATED invariant.
// Incremented when `__gt_drive_until` observes a GT `completed`, decremented when that struct is pushed onto the
// free-list; the exit leak gate (`MmRuntime.buildMmLeakCheck`, under `usesGt`) ORs it into its `!= 0` verdict, so a
// completed-but-unreclaimed struct exits 101 exactly as an unfreed heap object does. EXPORTED because that gate (a
// separate subsystem) reads it. It is COMPLETION-based, NOT alloc-based, on purpose: a never-awaited GT (spawned,
// never run — `spawn-not-awaited`, `store-overflow-aborts`) must exit with its OWN code, and an alloc-time counter
// would make it 101 (that never-run-GT leak is OPEN #88, out of scope). A completion counter never counts one.
export let GtLiveCountLabel = b"__gt_live_count"

// P1.5 mid-body-yield timer store: the live entry COUNT and the base address of the `__slab_alloc`'d heap
// region (both 0 until `__gt_init` allocates the region and seeds the base).
let GtTimerCountLabel = b"__gt_timer_count"
let GtTimerHeapBaseLabel = b"__gt_timer_heap_base"

// Single-M scratch words the QueryPerformanceCounter/Frequency out-params write to (`osReadClock` hands their
// addresses to the imports; `__gt_now_ns` reads them straight back). Safe as shared globals because
// `__gt_now_ns` is straight-line with no yield point inside it — it cannot be re-entered mid-read.
let GtClockTicksLabel = b"__gt_clock_ticks"
let GtClockFreqLabel = b"__gt_clock_freq"

// P1.5 subprocess store: the live parked-child COUNT and the base addresses of the two `__slab_alloc`'d parallel
// arrays (handles + waiting GTs). All 0 until `__gt_init` allocates the regions and seeds the bases.
let GtProcCountLabel = b"__gt_proc_count"
let GtProcHandlesBaseLabel = b"__gt_proc_handles_base"
let GtProcGtsBaseLabel = b"__gt_proc_gts_base"

// ⭐ P1.5-B1c #92 — the REUSABLE subprocess scratch buffers. `__gt_process_run` used to `__slab_alloc` four
// scratch buffers PER CALL (STARTUPINFOA, PROCESS_INFORMATION, the mutable cmdline copy, the exit-code slot),
// none reclaimed — ~150 B leaked per call. The fixed-size three (startup 0x68, procInfo 0x18, exit-code 8) are
// now allocated ONCE in `__gt_init` and reused every call; each is written+consumed in a NON-YIELDING single-M
// window (the STARTUPINFOA/PROCESS_INFORMATION reads finish before the GT parks at `parkSwitch`; the exit-code
// slot is used only in the post-resume `reap` block), so no two concurrent `__gt_process_run` calls' scratch can
// coexist. This mirrors the reference's STACK-FRAME scratch (v1 `X64Backend.maxon:4930-4958`, auto-reclaimed on
// return); shv2's `__gt_process_run` is builder-built Std with no addressable stack local, so a reused one-time
// global — exactly the timer-heap / proc-array pattern already in `__gt_init` — is the shv2-appropriate form.
let GtStartupBaseLabel = b"__gt_startup_base"
let GtProcInfoBaseLabel = b"__gt_procinfo_base"
let GtExitCodeBaseLabel = b"__gt_exitcode_base"

// The mutable cmdline copy is VARIABLE-sized, so it is a GROW-ON-DEMAND global (base + capacity) rather than a
// one-time init alloc: `__gt_process_run` reuses the buffer when `cmdlineLen + 1 <= cap`, else `__slab_alloc`s a
// bigger one and updates both. The old buffer bump-"leaks", but only when a strictly-longer command than any
// before appears — bounded by the number of distinct increasing lengths, not per-call (a spawn-loop of one
// command allocates it ONCE). Both 0 until the first `__gt_process_run` grows the buffer.
let GtCmdlineBufBaseLabel = b"__gt_cmdline_buf_base"
let GtCmdlineBufCapLabel = b"__gt_cmdline_buf_cap"

// The prefix every GT runtime entry point shares — the "does this program spawn a green thread?" test.
let GtRuntimeCallPrefix = b"__gt_"

// ⭐ **DOES THIS CALLEE REACH THE GT SCHEDULER?** The `__gt_`-only test that decides whether the program
// carries the scheduler runtime + its `.data` globals + the three hand-assembled chunks. Deliberately
// narrower than MmRuntime's `isRuntimeCallee`: a `__gt_*` call implies the whole scheduler (spawn enqueues,
// await drives, both context-switch), and it also implies the HEAP (spawn `__slab_alloc`s the GT struct and
// the arg buffer), which is why `scanRuntimeUsage` turns on `usesHeap` alongside `usesGt`.
export function isGtRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return bytesStartWith(callee, prefix: GtRuntimeCallPrefix)
end 'isGtRuntimeCallee'

// The GT runtime's `.data` slots, appended to the user's globals before `layOut` orders them — ordinary
// i64 slots in every respect, exactly like MmRuntime's `runtimeGlobals()`.
export function gtRuntimeGlobals() returns DataSectionEntryArray
	var out = DataSectionEntryArray.create()
	out.push(DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(GtCurrentGtLabel), valueType: StdType.i64, value: 0))
	out.push(DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(GtRunQueueHeadLabel), valueType: StdType.i64, value: 0))
	out.push(DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(GtRunQueueTailLabel), valueType: StdType.i64, value: 0))
	out.push(DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(GtInitDoneLabel), valueType: StdType.i64, value: 0))
	out.push(DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(GtSystemStackTopLabel), valueType: StdType.i64, value: 0))
	// P1.5 mid-body-yield: the timer store's count + heap base, and the clock out-param scratch words.
	out.push(DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(GtTimerCountLabel), valueType: StdType.i64, value: 0))
	out.push(DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(GtTimerHeapBaseLabel), valueType: StdType.i64, value: 0))
	out.push(DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(GtClockTicksLabel), valueType: StdType.i64, value: 0))
	out.push(DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(GtClockFreqLabel), valueType: StdType.i64, value: 0))
	// P1.5 subprocess store: the parked-child count + the two parallel-array bases.
	out.push(DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(GtProcCountLabel), valueType: StdType.i64, value: 0))
	out.push(DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(GtProcHandlesBaseLabel), valueType: StdType.i64, value: 0))
	out.push(DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(GtProcGtsBaseLabel), valueType: StdType.i64, value: 0))
	// P1.5-B1c #87: the GT-struct free-list head + the completion-based live-struct counter the leak gate reads.
	out.push(DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(GtFreeListHeadLabel), valueType: StdType.i64, value: 0))
	out.push(DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(GtLiveCountLabel), valueType: StdType.i64, value: 0))
	// P1.5-B1c #92: the reusable subprocess scratch bases (startup/procInfo/exit-code) + the grow-on-demand
	// cmdline-copy buffer's base and capacity.
	out.push(DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(GtStartupBaseLabel), valueType: StdType.i64, value: 0))
	out.push(DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(GtProcInfoBaseLabel), valueType: StdType.i64, value: 0))
	out.push(DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(GtExitCodeBaseLabel), valueType: StdType.i64, value: 0))
	out.push(DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(GtCmdlineBufBaseLabel), valueType: StdType.i64, value: 0))
	out.push(DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(GtCmdlineBufCapLabel), valueType: StdType.i64, value: 0))
	return out
end 'gtRuntimeGlobals'

// Install the GT scheduler into the lowered Std module. Called only when `moduleUsesGt` held. The three
// hand-assembled chunks (`__gt_context_switch`, `__gt_trampoline`, `__gt_morestack`) are appended separately at
// the Target tier (X64GtRuntime.appendX64GtRuntimeChunks) — they are bytes, not Std ops.
export function installGtRuntime(module StdModule)
	buildGtInit(module)
	buildGtSpawn(module)
	buildGtEnqueue(module)
	buildGtDequeue(module)
	buildGtNowNs(module)
	buildGtTimerAdd(module)
	buildGtTimerEarliest(module)
	buildGtTimerCheck(module)
	buildGtDriveUntil(module)
	buildGtAwaitDriver(module, name: GtAwaitName, throwing: false)
	buildGtAwaitDriver(module, name: GtTryAwaitName, throwing: true)
	buildGtSleep(module)
	buildGtProcAdd(module)
	buildGtProcCount(module)
	buildGtProcCheck(module)
	buildGtProcessRun(module)
end 'installGtRuntime'

// A two-argument `ValueIdArray` — the shape `__gt_spawn(funcPtr, argBuf)` and `__gt_context_switch(from, to)`
// share. MmRuntime's `argsOf` is the one-argument twin; the zero-argument call (`__gt_init`, `__gt_dequeue`)
// passes `ValueIdArray.create()` directly.
function args2(first ValueId, second ValueId) returns ValueIdArray
	var args = ValueIdArray.create()
	args.push(first)
	args.push(second)
	return args
end 'args2'

// `__gt_init()` — one-time scheduler init, idempotent via `__gt_init_done`. Allocates GT0 (the struct that
// will hold the MAIN thread's execution context: the first `__gt_await`'s `__gt_context_switch(GT0, g)`
// saves main's real rsp/rbp into GT0, so GT0 IS main from then on) and publishes it as the current GT.
// GT0 has stackBase 0 — it runs on the real OS stack and is never freed.
function buildGtInit(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtInitName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var doInit = module.addBlock(func, label: b"doinit")
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done")

	let initDoneAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(initDoneAddr, label: GtInitDoneLabel))
	let initDone = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(initDone, addrId: initDoneAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let zeroConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(zeroConst, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let already = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.cmp(already, lhs: initDone, rhs: zeroConst, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(already, thenBlockId: doneBlock.id, elseBlockId: doInit.id))

	// GT0 = __slab_alloc(GtStructSize). Slab-zeroed, so status is `ready` and every pointer field is null;
	// GT0 needs no stack or initial frame (it is already running as main).
	let sizeConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.const(sizeConst, value: GtStructSize, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let gt0 = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.call(gt0, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(sizeConst)))
	// __gt_current_gt = GT0.
	let curAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.globalAddr(curAddr, label: GtCurrentGtLabel))
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(curAddr, offset: 0, valueId: gt0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	// The timer store's heap region: one `__slab_alloc` of TimerHeapBytes (slab-zeroed, so count starts at 0
	// entries), its base published to `__gt_timer_heap_base`. Done once here rather than lazily in
	// `__gt_timer_add` so the add path stays a straight append with no allocation check.
	let heapSizeConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.const(heapSizeConst, value: TimerHeapBytes, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let heapBase = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.call(heapBase, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(heapSizeConst)))
	let heapBaseAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.globalAddr(heapBaseAddr, label: GtTimerHeapBaseLabel))
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(heapBaseAddr, offset: 0, valueId: heapBase, valueType: StdType.i64))
	// The process store's two parallel arrays (handles + waiting GTs), each `__slab_alloc`'d once (slab-zeroed,
	// so the count starts at 0) with its base published — parallel to the timer heap above.
	let procRegionSizeConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.const(procRegionSizeConst, value: ProcStoreBytes, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let procHandlesBase = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.call(procHandlesBase, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(procRegionSizeConst)))
	let procHandlesBaseAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.globalAddr(procHandlesBaseAddr, label: GtProcHandlesBaseLabel))
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(procHandlesBaseAddr, offset: 0, valueId: procHandlesBase, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let procGtsBase = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.call(procGtsBase, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(procRegionSizeConst)))
	let procGtsBaseAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.globalAddr(procGtsBaseAddr, label: GtProcGtsBaseLabel))
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(procGtsBaseAddr, offset: 0, valueId: procGtsBase, valueType: StdType.i64))
	// The single 64 KB SYSTEM (scratch) stack the syscall shim runs kernel calls on (P1.5-B1a′). `osAllocPages`
	// (not `__slab_alloc`) so the region is its own PAGE-aligned commit — the top (`base + GtSystemStackSize`) is
	// then 16-aligned, which the shim relies on to land RSP 16-aligned at the `call`. Its top is published to
	// `__gt_system_stack_top`. This runs on GT0 (the main thread, `stackBase == 0`), so the `osAllocPages` here is
	// itself NOT shimmed — no chicken-and-egg where allocating the system stack would need the system stack.
	let sysStackSizeConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.const(sysStackSizeConst, value: GtSystemStackSize, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let sysStackBase = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.osAllocPages(sysStackBase, sizeId: sysStackSizeConst))
	let sysStackTop = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.binOp(sysStackTop, lhs: sysStackBase, rhs: sysStackSizeConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let sysStackTopAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.globalAddr(sysStackTopAddr, label: GtSystemStackTopLabel))
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(sysStackTopAddr, offset: 0, valueId: sysStackTop, valueType: StdType.i64))
	// P1.5-B1c #92: the three FIXED-size subprocess scratch buffers, `__slab_alloc`'d ONCE here and reused by
	// every `__gt_process_run` (they were per-call allocs that leaked ~150 B/call). STARTUPINFOA's `cb` field is
	// set here once — CreateProcessA only READS the struct, so the single init store holds for every call — and
	// the rest stay slab-zeroed. PROCESS_INFORMATION's `hProcess` (the spawn-failure sentinel) and the exit-code
	// slot are re-cleaned per call at their use site in `__gt_process_run`. Allocated unconditionally like the
	// timer heap / proc arrays above (a pure-async program that never runs a subprocess pays ~0x88 one-time bytes,
	// the same over-provisioning those regions already make). The variable cmdline copy is grown on demand there.
	let startupSizeConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.const(startupSizeConst, value: StartupInfoSize, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let startupBase = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.call(startupBase, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(startupSizeConst)))
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(startupBase, offset: StartupInfoCbOffset, valueId: startupSizeConst, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let startupBaseAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.globalAddr(startupBaseAddr, label: GtStartupBaseLabel))
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(startupBaseAddr, offset: 0, valueId: startupBase, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let procInfoSizeConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.const(procInfoSizeConst, value: ProcInfoSize, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let procInfoBase = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.call(procInfoBase, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(procInfoSizeConst)))
	let procInfoBaseAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.globalAddr(procInfoBaseAddr, label: GtProcInfoBaseLabel))
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(procInfoBaseAddr, offset: 0, valueId: procInfoBase, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let exitCodeSizeConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.const(exitCodeSizeConst, value: ExitCodeSlotBytes, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let exitCodeBase = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.call(exitCodeBase, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(exitCodeSizeConst)))
	let exitCodeBaseAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.globalAddr(exitCodeBaseAddr, label: GtExitCodeBaseLabel))
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(exitCodeBaseAddr, offset: 0, valueId: exitCodeBase, valueType: StdType.i64))
	// __gt_init_done = 1.
	let oneConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.const(oneConst, value: 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(doInit, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(initDoneAddr, offset: 0, valueId: oneConst, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(doInit, op: StdOp.branch(doneBlock.id))

	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.retVoid)
	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtInit'

// `__gt_spawn(funcPtr) -> gt` — create a ready green thread and enqueue it. Obtains a GT struct (a recycled one
// off the free-list, else a fresh `__slab_alloc`), commits its seed stack, lays the initial context-switch frame
// so the first switch-in `ret`s into the trampoline, points `gt.argBuf` at the struct's OWN inline arg region,
// and enqueues the GT. Returns the GT pointer as the promise handle.
//
// ⭐ P1.5-B1c #87 — THE ARG BUFFER IS NO LONGER A PARAMETER. It lives INLINE at `gt + GtOffArgInline` (the slots
// the lowering fills after this returns, safe because a spawned GT does not run until awaited); `gt.argBuf` is
// set to that self-pointer, which the trampoline dereferences exactly as it read a separate buffer. So the
// separate per-spawn `__slab_alloc`'d arg buffer — and its leak — is gone. `argCount` is still not a parameter:
// the region is a fixed MaxAsyncArgs slots and the trampoline loads all of them (see the header).
//
// ⭐ P1.5-B1c #87 — REUSE A COMPLETED STRUCT. If `__gt_free_list_head` is non-null, POP it and WHOLE-STRUCT
// MEMZERO it before re-init; else fall back to a fresh `__slab_alloc` (already slab-zeroed, so it skips the
// memzero). The zero-on-recycle is a CORRECTNESS TRAP, not an optimization: a free-list struct lives OUTSIDE the
// always-zeroing slab, so a recycled one still carries its previous tenant's status/result/waiter/io_*/threw/tib
// fields — v1 fixed exactly this class of bug (a spurious pipe-read abort, `Arm64MacosGreenThread.maxon:2376-2404`)
// with a whole-struct memzero on the recycle arm; the bootstrap's field-by-field re-init is the WEAKER version.
//
// It does NOT run the GT — a spawned GT runs only when a driver (`__gt_await`) dequeues and switches into it.
function buildGtSpawn(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtSpawnName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var freshAlloc = module.addBlock(func, label: b"freshalloc")
	var reuse = module.addBlock(func, label: b"reuse")
	var zeroHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"zerohdr")
	var zeroBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"zerobody")
	var initBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"init")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let funcPtr = 0 as ValueId

	// Lazy one-time scheduler init. A void call still mints an (unread) result id, as `__mm_decref`'s
	// `__mm_free` call does.
	let initResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(initResult, callee: GtInitName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))

	// Reuse a completed struct if the free-list is non-empty, else allocate a fresh (already-zeroed) one.
	let structSize = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(structSize, value: GtStructSize, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let zeroConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(zeroConst, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let freeHeadAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(freeHeadAddr, label: GtFreeListHeadLabel))
	let freeHead = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(freeHead, addrId: freeHeadAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let freeEmpty = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.cmp(freeEmpty, lhs: freeHead, rhs: zeroConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(freeEmpty, thenBlockId: freshAlloc.id, elseBlockId: reuse.id))

	// freshAlloc: gt = __slab_alloc(GtStructSize) — slab-zeroed, so no memzero needed → init(gtFresh).
	let gtFresh = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(freshAlloc, op: StdOp.call(gtFresh, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(structSize)))
	module.setTerminator(freshAlloc, op: StdOp.branch(initBlock.id))
	freshAlloc.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(initBlock.id, argIds: argsOf(gtFresh)))

	// reuse: pop the head off the free-list (`__gt_free_list_head = freeHead.next`), then whole-struct memzero it.
	let poppedNext = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(reuse, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(poppedNext, addrId: freeHead, offset: GtOffNext, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(reuse, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(freeHeadAddr, offset: 0, valueId: poppedNext, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(reuse, op: StdOp.branch(zeroHdr.id))
	reuse.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(zeroHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(zeroConst)))

	// zeroHdr(off): if off >= GtStructSize → init(freeHead) (whole struct now zeroed), else zero the word at `off`.
	let off = ids.mint()
	zeroHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(off, argType: StdType.i64))
	let zeroDone = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(zeroHdr, op: StdOp.cmp(zeroDone, lhs: off, rhs: structSize, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(zeroHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(zeroDone, thenBlockId: initBlock.id, elseBlockId: zeroBody.id))
	zeroHdr.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(initBlock.id, argIds: argsOf(freeHead)))

	// zeroBody: *(freeHead + off) = 0 ; off += 8 → zeroHdr.
	let slotAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(zeroBody, op: StdOp.binOp(slotAddr, lhs: freeHead, rhs: off, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(zeroBody, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(slotAddr, offset: 0, valueId: zeroConst, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let wordBytesConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(zeroBody, op: StdOp.const(wordBytesConst, value: GtArgSlotBytes, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let offNext = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(zeroBody, op: StdOp.binOp(offNext, lhs: off, rhs: wordBytesConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(zeroBody, op: StdOp.branch(zeroHdr.id))
	zeroBody.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(zeroHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(offNext)))

	// init(gt): the shared init for both the fresh and recycled struct — now guaranteed zeroed either way.
	let gt = ids.mint()
	initBlock.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(gt, argType: StdType.i64))

	// gt.argBuf = gt + GtOffArgInline — a SELF-POINTER into this struct's own inline arg region (P1.5-B1c #87).
	// The trampoline reads `gt.argBuf` as a pointer and loads the slots from there, so pointing it at the inline
	// region makes the buffer part of the struct — reclaimed for free when the struct is recycled.
	let argInlineOffConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.const(argInlineOffConst, value: GtOffArgInline, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let argInlinePtr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.binOp(argInlinePtr, lhs: gt, rhs: argInlineOffConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(gt, offset: GtOffArgBuf, valueId: argInlinePtr, valueType: StdType.i64))

	// ⭐ COMMIT A FRESH GtStackSize STACK (P1.5-B1a′). The STACK is variable-sized (the relocating morestack grows
	// it), so it is NOT recycled with the struct — a completed GT's stack is RELEASED (`__gt_drive_until`) and a
	// fresh seed is committed here every spawn (`MEM_COMMIT`, demand-paged so untouched pages cost nothing).
	let stackSizeVal = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.const(stackSizeVal, value: GtStackSize, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let stackBase = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.osAllocPages(stackBase, sizeId: stackSizeVal))
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(gt, offset: GtOffStackBase, valueId: stackBase, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(gt, offset: GtOffStackSize, valueId: stackSizeVal, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(gt, offset: GtOffFuncPtr, valueId: funcPtr, valueType: StdType.i64))

	// stackTop = stackBase + GtStackSize.
	let stackTop = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.binOp(stackTop, lhs: stackBase, rhs: stackSizeVal, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	// Seed this GT's saved TIB bounds so the syscall shim's after-call restore repoints the Windows TIB to THIS
	// GT's own stack: `tibStackBase = stackTop` (high address), `tibStackLimit = stackBase` (low). The GT's bounds
	// are `[stackBase, stackTop)`; `__gt_morestack` updates them (and the guard) when it grows the stack.
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(gt, offset: GtOffTibStackBase, valueId: stackTop, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(gt, offset: GtOffTibStackLimit, valueId: stackBase, valueType: StdType.i64))
	// gt.stackGuard = stackBase + GtStackGuardMargin — the low-water mark the guarded function's prologue compares
	// its projected rsp against (P1.5-B1a′). Below it, the prologue calls `__gt_morestack` to grow+relocate first.
	let guardMarginConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.const(guardMarginConst, value: GtStackGuardMargin, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let stackGuard = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.binOp(stackGuard, lhs: stackBase, rhs: guardMarginConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(gt, offset: GtOffStackGuard, valueId: stackGuard, valueType: StdType.i64))
	// [stackTop - 8] = &__gt_trampoline (the return address the first `ret` pops). The five callee-saved slots below
	// it are the MEM_COMMIT zeros — DON'T-CARE, because they become this GT's initial callee-saved registers, which
	// the user function establishes before it reads them. Only the trampoline return-address slot must be written.
	let eightConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.const(eightConst, value: 8, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let retSlotAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.binOp(retSlotAddr, lhs: stackTop, rhs: eightConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let trampAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.funcAddr(trampAddr, funcName: GtTrampolineName))
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(retSlotAddr, offset: 0, valueId: trampAddr, valueType: StdType.i64))
	// gt.sp = stackTop - GtInitialFrameBytes (points at the lowest saved-register slot); gt.fp is 0 (zeroed).
	let frameConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.const(frameConst, value: GtInitialFrameBytes, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let spVal = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.binOp(spVal, lhs: stackTop, rhs: frameConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(gt, offset: GtOffSp, valueId: spVal, valueType: StdType.i64))

	// Enqueue as ready and return the GT as the promise handle.
	let enqResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.call(enqResult, callee: GtEnqueueName, args: argsOf(gt)))
	module.setTerminator(initBlock, op: StdOp.ret(gt))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtSpawn'

// `__gt_enqueue(g)` — append `g` to the tail of the global FIFO run queue.
function buildGtEnqueue(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtEnqueueName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var appendBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"append")
	var emptyBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"empty")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let g = 0 as ValueId

	// g.next = 0.
	let zeroConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(zeroConst, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(g, offset: GtOffNext, valueId: zeroConst, valueType: StdType.i64))

	let tailAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(tailAddr, label: GtRunQueueTailLabel))
	let tail = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(tail, addrId: tailAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let tailNull = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.cmp(tailNull, lhs: tail, rhs: zeroConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(tailNull, thenBlockId: emptyBlock.id, elseBlockId: appendBlock.id))

	// Non-empty: tail.next = g; tail = g.
	module.appendOp(appendBlock, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(tail, offset: GtOffNext, valueId: g, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(appendBlock, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(tailAddr, offset: 0, valueId: g, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(appendBlock, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	// Empty: head = tail = g.
	let headAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(emptyBlock, op: StdOp.globalAddr(headAddr, label: GtRunQueueHeadLabel))
	module.appendOp(emptyBlock, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(headAddr, offset: 0, valueId: g, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(emptyBlock, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(tailAddr, offset: 0, valueId: g, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(emptyBlock, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtEnqueue'

// `__gt_dequeue() -> g` — pop the head green thread off the global FIFO run queue, or 0 if empty.
function buildGtDequeue(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtDequeueName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var popBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pop")
	var clearTail = module.addBlock(func, label: b"cleartail")
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done")

	let headAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(headAddr, label: GtRunQueueHeadLabel))
	let head = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(head, addrId: headAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let zeroConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(zeroConst, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let headNull = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.cmp(headNull, lhs: head, rhs: zeroConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	// head is minted in entry (dominates every block), so `done` returns it in both cases: 0 when empty,
	// the popped GT otherwise.
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(headNull, thenBlockId: doneBlock.id, elseBlockId: popBlock.id))

	// head = head.next; if the new head is null the queue is empty → clear the tail too.
	let next = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(popBlock, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(next, addrId: head, offset: GtOffNext, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(popBlock, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(headAddr, offset: 0, valueId: next, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let nextNull = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(popBlock, op: StdOp.cmp(nextNull, lhs: next, rhs: zeroConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(popBlock, op: StdOp.condBranch(nextNull, thenBlockId: clearTail.id, elseBlockId: doneBlock.id))

	let tailAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(clearTail, op: StdOp.globalAddr(tailAddr, label: GtRunQueueTailLabel))
	module.appendOp(clearTail, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(tailAddr, offset: 0, valueId: zeroConst, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(clearTail, op: StdOp.branch(doneBlock.id))

	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.ret(head))
	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtDequeue'

// `__gt_now_ns() -> i64` — the monotonic clock, nanoseconds since boot. `osReadClock` reads the QPC counter
// and QPF frequency into two `.data` scratch words; the ticks→ns scale is done HERE in builder-built Std, via
// the OVERFLOW-SAFE split identity `ns = (ticks/freq)*1e9 + ((ticks mod freq)*1e9)/freq`. Both terms stay
// inside i64 for any realistic QPF (Windows fixes it at 10 MHz; even a TSC-derived few-GHz value keeps
// `(freq-1)*1e9 < 2^63`) and centuries of uptime — so no 128-bit MUL/DIV, and no new Target op, is needed.
function buildGtNowNs(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtNowNsName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")

	let freqAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(freqAddr, label: GtClockFreqLabel))
	let ticksAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(ticksAddr, label: GtClockTicksLabel))
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.osReadClock(ticksAddr, freqPtrId: freqAddr))
	let ticks = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(ticks, addrId: ticksAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let freq = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(freq, addrId: freqAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))

	let billion = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(billion, value: NanosPerSecond, valueType: StdType.i64))
	// wholeSecs = ticks / freq ; wholeNs = wholeSecs * 1e9.
	let wholeSecs = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.div(wholeSecs, lhs: ticks, rhs: freq))
	let wholeNs = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.binOp(wholeNs, lhs: wholeSecs, rhs: billion, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, operandType: StdType.i64))
	// fracNs = ((ticks mod freq) * 1e9) / freq — the sub-second remainder, exact and overflow-free.
	let rem = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.mod(rem, lhs: ticks, rhs: freq))
	let remNs = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.binOp(remNs, lhs: rem, rhs: billion, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let fracNs = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.div(fracNs, lhs: remNs, rhs: freq))
	let ns = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.binOp(ns, lhs: wholeNs, rhs: fracNs, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.ret(ns))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtNowNs'

// `__gt_timer_add(gt, deadline)` — append a parked GT's (deadline, gt) entry to the timer store. An UNSORTED
// append (see the store's header note on the min-heap deviation): store the pair at `heap[count]`, count++.
// No capacity guard: the concurrent-timer count is bounded by simultaneously-sleeping GTs (≤2 in the specs,
// 1 in a sequential loop), far below `TimerHeapCapacity`.
function buildGtTimerAdd(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtTimerAddName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let gt = 0 as ValueId
	let deadline = 1 as ValueId

	let countAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(countAddr, label: GtTimerCountLabel))
	let count = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(count, addrId: countAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let heapBaseAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(heapBaseAddr, label: GtTimerHeapBaseLabel))
	let heapBase = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(heapBase, addrId: heapBaseAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	// &heap[count] = heapBase + count * TimerEntrySize.
	let entrySizeConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(entrySizeConst, value: TimerEntrySize, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let entryOff = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.binOp(entryOff, lhs: count, rhs: entrySizeConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let entryAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.binOp(entryAddr, lhs: heapBase, rhs: entryOff, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(entryAddr, offset: TimerOffDeadline, valueId: deadline, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(entryAddr, offset: TimerOffGt, valueId: gt, valueType: StdType.i64))
	// count++.
	let oneConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(oneConst, value: 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let newCount = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.binOp(newCount, lhs: count, rhs: oneConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(countAddr, offset: 0, valueId: newCount, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtTimerAdd'

// `__gt_timer_earliest() -> i64` — the smallest deadline in the store, or `TimerNoDeadline` (0) when empty.
// A linear min-scan (the unsorted store makes peek-min O(n); n is tiny). The netpoll uses it to size its
// `Sleep`, and an empty store there means "no parked GT and the run queue is empty" — a deadlock the caller
// bails on.
function buildGtTimerEarliest(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtTimerEarliestName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var initBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"init")
	var scanHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"scanhdr")
	var scanBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"scanbody")
	var takeCur = module.addBlock(func, label: b"takecur")
	var keepBest = module.addBlock(func, label: b"keepbest")
	var emptyBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"empty")
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done")

	let countAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(countAddr, label: GtTimerCountLabel))
	let count = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(count, addrId: countAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let zeroConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(zeroConst, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let isEmpty = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.cmp(isEmpty, lhs: count, rhs: zeroConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(isEmpty, thenBlockId: emptyBlock.id, elseBlockId: initBlock.id))

	// init: best = heap[0].deadline; enter the scan at i = 1.
	let heapBaseAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.globalAddr(heapBaseAddr, label: GtTimerHeapBaseLabel))
	let heapBase = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(heapBase, addrId: heapBaseAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let best0 = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(best0, addrId: heapBase, offset: TimerOffDeadline, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let oneConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.const(oneConst, value: 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(initBlock, op: StdOp.branch(scanHdr.id))

	// scanHdr(i, best): if i >= count → done(best), else → scanBody.
	let i = ids.mint()
	let best = ids.mint()
	scanHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(i, argType: StdType.i64))
	scanHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(best, argType: StdType.i64))
	initBlock.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(scanHdr.id, argIds: args2(oneConst, second: best0)))
	let atEnd = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(scanHdr, op: StdOp.cmp(atEnd, lhs: i, rhs: count, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(scanHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(atEnd, thenBlockId: doneBlock.id, elseBlockId: scanBody.id))

	// scanBody: di = heap[i].deadline; iNext = i+1; if di < best → takeCur, else → keepBest.
	let entrySizeConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(scanBody, op: StdOp.const(entrySizeConst, value: TimerEntrySize, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let iOff = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(scanBody, op: StdOp.binOp(iOff, lhs: i, rhs: entrySizeConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let entryAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(scanBody, op: StdOp.binOp(entryAddr, lhs: heapBase, rhs: iOff, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let di = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(scanBody, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(di, addrId: entryAddr, offset: TimerOffDeadline, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let oneStep = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(scanBody, op: StdOp.const(oneStep, value: 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let iNext = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(scanBody, op: StdOp.binOp(iNext, lhs: i, rhs: oneStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let isLess = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(scanBody, op: StdOp.cmp(isLess, lhs: di, rhs: best, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(scanBody, op: StdOp.condBranch(isLess, thenBlockId: takeCur.id, elseBlockId: keepBest.id))

	// takeCur: best' = di. keepBest: best' = best. Both loop to scanHdr(iNext, best').
	module.setTerminator(takeCur, op: StdOp.branch(scanHdr.id))
	takeCur.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(scanHdr.id, argIds: args2(iNext, second: di)))
	module.setTerminator(keepBest, op: StdOp.branch(scanHdr.id))
	keepBest.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(scanHdr.id, argIds: args2(iNext, second: best)))

	// done(best): return the minimum. empty: return the sentinel.
	let doneBest = ids.mint()
	doneBlock.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(doneBest, argType: StdType.i64))
	scanHdr.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(doneBlock.id, argIds: argsOf(best)))
	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.ret(doneBest))

	let emptyResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(emptyBlock, op: StdOp.const(emptyResult, value: TimerNoDeadline, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(emptyBlock, op: StdOp.ret(emptyResult))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtTimerEarliest'

// `__gt_timer_check(now)` — fire every parked GT whose deadline has passed: set its status to `ready`,
// re-enqueue it (UNLESS it is `__gt_current_gt` — a self-driving GT0 polling its OWN deadline is already on
// its stack, so status=ready alone wakes it and enqueueing would double-schedule it), and remove its entry
// from the store (swap the last entry into the hole, count--; re-examine the same index). Deadline order is
// preserved by the netpoll waking on the earliest deadline first, so the shorter sleeper resumes first.
function buildGtTimerCheck(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtTimerCheckName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var loopHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"loophdr")
	var loopBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"loopbody")
	var advance = module.addBlock(func, label: b"advance")
	var fire = module.addBlock(func, label: b"fire")
	var doEnqueue = module.addBlock(func, label: b"enqueue")
	var afterFire = module.addBlock(func, label: b"afterfire")
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let now = 0 as ValueId

	let countAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(countAddr, label: GtTimerCountLabel))
	let count0 = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(count0, addrId: countAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let heapBaseAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(heapBaseAddr, label: GtTimerHeapBaseLabel))
	let heapBase = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(heapBase, addrId: heapBaseAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let zeroConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(zeroConst, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(loopHdr.id))

	// loopHdr(i, count): if i >= count → done(count), else → loopBody.
	let i = ids.mint()
	let count = ids.mint()
	loopHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(i, argType: StdType.i64))
	loopHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(count, argType: StdType.i64))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(loopHdr.id, argIds: args2(zeroConst, second: count0)))
	let atEnd = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(loopHdr, op: StdOp.cmp(atEnd, lhs: i, rhs: count, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(loopHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(atEnd, thenBlockId: doneBlock.id, elseBlockId: loopBody.id))

	// loopBody: di = heap[i].deadline; if di <= now → fire, else → advance.
	let entrySizeConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(loopBody, op: StdOp.const(entrySizeConst, value: TimerEntrySize, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let iOff = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(loopBody, op: StdOp.binOp(iOff, lhs: i, rhs: entrySizeConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let entryAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(loopBody, op: StdOp.binOp(entryAddr, lhs: heapBase, rhs: iOff, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let di = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(loopBody, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(di, addrId: entryAddr, offset: TimerOffDeadline, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let isDue = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(loopBody, op: StdOp.cmp(isDue, lhs: di, rhs: now, pred: StdCmpPred.le, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(loopBody, op: StdOp.condBranch(isDue, thenBlockId: fire.id, elseBlockId: advance.id))

	// advance: not due → loopHdr(i+1, count).
	let oneAdv = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(advance, op: StdOp.const(oneAdv, value: 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let iAdv = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(advance, op: StdOp.binOp(iAdv, lhs: i, rhs: oneAdv, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(advance, op: StdOp.branch(loopHdr.id))
	advance.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(loopHdr.id, argIds: args2(iAdv, second: count)))

	// fire: gt = heap[i].gt; gt.status = ready; skip enqueue when gt is the current (self-driving) GT.
	let fireEntrySizeConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(fire, op: StdOp.const(fireEntrySizeConst, value: TimerEntrySize, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let fireOff = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(fire, op: StdOp.binOp(fireOff, lhs: i, rhs: fireEntrySizeConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let fireEntryAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(fire, op: StdOp.binOp(fireEntryAddr, lhs: heapBase, rhs: fireOff, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let gt = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(fire, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(gt, addrId: fireEntryAddr, offset: TimerOffGt, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let readyConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(fire, op: StdOp.const(readyConst, value: GtStatusReady, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(fire, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(gt, offset: GtOffStatus, valueId: readyConst, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let curAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(fire, op: StdOp.globalAddr(curAddr, label: GtCurrentGtLabel))
	let curGt = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(fire, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(curGt, addrId: curAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let isCurrent = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(fire, op: StdOp.cmp(isCurrent, lhs: gt, rhs: curGt, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(fire, op: StdOp.condBranch(isCurrent, thenBlockId: afterFire.id, elseBlockId: doEnqueue.id))

	// doEnqueue: put the woken GT back on the run queue.
	let enqResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doEnqueue, op: StdOp.call(enqResult, callee: GtEnqueueName, args: argsOf(gt)))
	module.setTerminator(doEnqueue, op: StdOp.branch(afterFire.id))

	// afterFire: remove entry i — copy the last live entry over it, count--, re-examine i (don't advance).
	let oneRem = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterFire, op: StdOp.const(oneRem, value: 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let newCount = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterFire, op: StdOp.binOp(newCount, lhs: count, rhs: oneRem, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let afEntrySize = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterFire, op: StdOp.const(afEntrySize, value: TimerEntrySize, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let lastOff = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterFire, op: StdOp.binOp(lastOff, lhs: newCount, rhs: afEntrySize, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let lastAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterFire, op: StdOp.binOp(lastAddr, lhs: heapBase, rhs: lastOff, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let curOff = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterFire, op: StdOp.binOp(curOff, lhs: i, rhs: afEntrySize, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let holeAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterFire, op: StdOp.binOp(holeAddr, lhs: heapBase, rhs: curOff, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	// Copy the last entry (deadline, gt) into the hole. A self-copy when i == newCount is harmless.
	let movedDeadline = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterFire, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(movedDeadline, addrId: lastAddr, offset: TimerOffDeadline, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let movedGt = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterFire, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(movedGt, addrId: lastAddr, offset: TimerOffGt, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(afterFire, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(holeAddr, offset: TimerOffDeadline, valueId: movedDeadline, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(afterFire, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(holeAddr, offset: TimerOffGt, valueId: movedGt, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(afterFire, op: StdOp.branch(loopHdr.id))
	afterFire.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(loopHdr.id, argIds: args2(i, second: newCount)))

	// done(count): publish the surviving entry count.
	let doneCount = ids.mint()
	doneBlock.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(doneCount, argType: StdType.i64))
	loopHdr.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(doneBlock.id, argIds: argsOf(count)))
	let doneCountAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doneBlock, op: StdOp.globalAddr(doneCountAddr, label: GtTimerCountLabel))
	module.appendOp(doneBlock, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(doneCountAddr, offset: 0, valueId: doneCount, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtTimerCheck'

// Emit `ceil((earliest - now) / 1e6)` into `block` and return the deltaMs id — the millisecond delta both the
// timer-only Sleep and the timer-bounded process wait block for. Rounding UP guarantees ≥1 ms, so a
// sub-millisecond remainder cannot busy-spin. Factored so the two netpoll blocks that compute it stay one fact.
function emitCeilMs(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, earliest ValueId, now ValueId) returns ValueId
	let delta = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.binOp(delta, lhs: earliest, rhs: now, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let milliMinusOne = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.const(milliMinusOne, value: NanosPerMilli - 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let deltaRounded = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.binOp(deltaRounded, lhs: delta, rhs: milliMinusOne, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let milliConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.const(milliConst, value: NanosPerMilli, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let deltaMs = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.div(deltaMs, lhs: deltaRounded, rhs: milliConst))
	return deltaMs
end 'emitCeilMs'

// Emit the netpoll's single-M block on the parked children into `block`: `WaitForMultipleObjects(procHandles, pc,
// FALSE, timeoutMs)` then `__gt_proc_check()` to wake every child that has since exited. `timeoutMs` is INFINITE
// when no timer bounds the wait, else the timer delta. The handle array is the store's `procHandles` array itself
// — parallel-array storage keeps it contiguous, so no gather copy is needed. Factored so the two blocks that
// block on children (bounded and unbounded) share one spelling of the wait+recheck.
function emitProcWait(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, pc ValueId, timeoutMs ValueId)
	let handlesBaseAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.globalAddr(handlesBaseAddr, label: GtProcHandlesBaseLabel))
	let handlesBase = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(handlesBase, addrId: handlesBaseAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.osWaitHandles(handlesBase, countId: pc, timeoutMsId: timeoutMs))
	let checkResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(checkResult, callee: GtProcCheckName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
end 'emitProcWait'

// ⭐ `__gt_drive_until(targetGt, targetStatus)` — THE SHARED COOPERATIVE DRIVE, and the only place a switch is
// driven. `selfGt` = the running driver (`__gt_current_gt`: GT0 for a top-level await/sleep, a nested GT for
// a nested one). It loops: dequeue a ready GT, publish it, switch into it, and on switch-back RECYCLE its
// stack ONLY IF it `completed` (a GT that yielded mid-body via `__gt_sleep`/`__gt_process_run` is `waiting`, not
// `completed`, so its stack is KEPT — recycling a yielded GT's stack is a use-after-free when it resumes, the #1
// trap). When the run queue empties, NETPOLL: wake any due timers / exited children, else BLOCK the single M on
// whichever is pending — `Sleep` on the earliest timer, `WaitForMultipleObjects` on the parked children (bounded
// by the earliest timer if both are pending), never a busy-spin — and re-poll. Runs UNTIL `targetGt.status ==
// targetStatus`.
//
// `__gt_await(p)` drives until the promise's GT is `completed`; a top-level `__gt_sleep`/`__gt_process_run` drives
// until its own GT is `ready` (woken by its timer / its child's exit). The loop state lives entirely in memory
// (the current GT, the run queue, the timer + process stores), so this is phi-free — each iteration reloads it.
function buildGtDriveUntil(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtDriveUntilName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var loopHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"loophdr")
	var drive = module.addBlock(func, label: b"drive")
	var run = module.addBlock(func, label: b"run")
	var recycle = module.addBlock(func, label: b"recycle")
	var freeStack = module.addBlock(func, label: b"freestack")
	var pushFree = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pushfree")
	var netpoll = module.addBlock(func, label: b"netpoll")
	var timerOnly = module.addBlock(func, label: b"timeronly")
	var timerDecide = module.addBlock(func, label: b"timerdecide")
	var firenow = module.addBlock(func, label: b"firenow")
	var sleepwait = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sleepwait")
	var haveProcs = module.addBlock(func, label: b"haveprocs")
	var procInfinite = module.addBlock(func, label: b"procinfinite")
	var procTimerDecide = module.addBlock(func, label: b"proctimerdecide")
	var procWaitBounded = module.addBlock(func, label: b"procwaitbounded")
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let targetGt = 0 as ValueId
	let targetStatus = 1 as ValueId

	let curAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(curAddr, label: GtCurrentGtLabel))
	let selfGt = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(selfGt, addrId: curAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let zeroConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(zeroConst, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(loopHdr.id))

	// loophdr: if targetGt.status == targetStatus → done, else → drive.
	let status = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(loopHdr, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(status, addrId: targetGt, offset: GtOffStatus, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let reached = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(loopHdr, op: StdOp.cmp(reached, lhs: status, rhs: targetStatus, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(loopHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(reached, thenBlockId: doneBlock.id, elseBlockId: drive.id))

	// drive: g = __gt_dequeue(); if g == 0 → netpoll, else → run.
	let g = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(drive, op: StdOp.call(g, callee: GtDequeueName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	let gNull = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(drive, op: StdOp.cmp(gNull, lhs: g, rhs: zeroConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(drive, op: StdOp.condBranch(gNull, thenBlockId: netpoll.id, elseBlockId: run.id))

	// run: g.waiter = selfGt; current = g; switch into g; on switch-back restore current, then gate recycle.
	module.appendOp(run, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(g, offset: GtOffWaiter, valueId: selfGt, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(run, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(curAddr, offset: 0, valueId: g, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let switchResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(run, op: StdOp.call(switchResult, callee: GtContextSwitchName, args: args2(selfGt, second: g)))
	module.appendOp(run, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(curAddr, offset: 0, valueId: selfGt, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let gStatus = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(run, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(gStatus, addrId: g, offset: GtOffStatus, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let completedConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(run, op: StdOp.const(completedConst, value: GtStatusCompleted, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let gCompleted = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(run, op: StdOp.cmp(gCompleted, lhs: gStatus, rhs: completedConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	// ⭐ RECYCLE ONLY A COMPLETED g. A merely-parked (waiting) g keeps its stack — it will resume on it.
	module.setTerminator(run, op: StdOp.condBranch(gCompleted, thenBlockId: recycle.id, elseBlockId: loopHdr.id))

	// recycle: g completed → its (possibly grown) stack is idle. Skip GT0/main (stackBase 0, runs on the OS stack
	// and is never freed); else RELEASE it. `osFreePages` is an `iatCall` inside this `__gt_` function, so slice 1's
	// syscall shim wraps it onto the system stack automatically — the `VirtualFree` runs off the driver's own GT
	// stack, and it releases g's stack (a different region), which g has already switched off of.
	//
	// ⭐ P1.5-B1c #87: g's struct is now LIVE-UNTIL-RECLAIMED — count it. This block is entered EXACTLY when g is
	// `completed`, so the increment is per-completion (never for a never-run/never-awaited GT, which is why the
	// counter is completion-based, not alloc-based). It is decremented in `pushFree` when g goes onto the free-list;
	// the exit leak gate (`MmRuntime.buildMmLeakCheck`, under usesGt) ORs `__gt_live_count` into its `!= 0` verdict,
	// so a completed-but-unreclaimed struct exits 101 exactly as an unfreed heap object does.
	let liveIncAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(recycle, op: StdOp.globalAddr(liveIncAddr, label: GtLiveCountLabel))
	let liveIncOld = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(recycle, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(liveIncOld, addrId: liveIncAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let liveIncOne = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(recycle, op: StdOp.const(liveIncOne, value: 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let liveIncNew = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(recycle, op: StdOp.binOp(liveIncNew, lhs: liveIncOld, rhs: liveIncOne, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(recycle, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(liveIncAddr, offset: 0, valueId: liveIncNew, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let gStackBase = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(recycle, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(gStackBase, addrId: g, offset: GtOffStackBase, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let gStackNull = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(recycle, op: StdOp.cmp(gStackNull, lhs: gStackBase, rhs: zeroConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	// GT0 (stackBase 0) skips the stack free but STILL recycles its struct — `pushFree` reclaims it (correctness:
	// GT0 never completes, so this arm is unreached today, but the recycle path stays whole either way).
	module.setTerminator(recycle, op: StdOp.condBranch(gStackNull, thenBlockId: pushFree.id, elseBlockId: freeStack.id))

	// freeStack: release g's completed stack (whatever size the morestack grew it to — `VirtualFree(MEM_RELEASE)`
	// frees the whole reservation from its base). This bounds a spawn/await loop by alloc+free churn. Then the
	// struct is reclaimed onto the free-list in `pushFree`.
	module.appendOp(freeStack, op: StdOp.osFreePages(gStackBase))
	module.setTerminator(freeStack, op: StdOp.branch(pushFree.id))

	// ⭐ pushFree: RECLAIM g's completed struct onto the free-list (P1.5-B1c #87) — `g.next = __gt_free_list_head`;
	// `__gt_free_list_head = g` (a LIFO push through the now-idle run-queue link, since a completed g is off the run
	// queue). `__gt_spawn` pops it before falling back to a fresh `__slab_alloc`, so a spawn/await loop reuses ONE
	// struct rather than bump-leaking one per iteration. Single-owner: only this driver reclaims a GT in single-M
	// (there is no competing drain path), so the push races nothing. Decrement `__gt_live_count` here — the struct
	// is now reclaimed, balancing the increment `recycle` made on completion, so a clean spawn/await loop nets zero.
	let freeHeadAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(pushFree, op: StdOp.globalAddr(freeHeadAddr, label: GtFreeListHeadLabel))
	let oldFreeHead = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(pushFree, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(oldFreeHead, addrId: freeHeadAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(pushFree, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(g, offset: GtOffNext, valueId: oldFreeHead, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(pushFree, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(freeHeadAddr, offset: 0, valueId: g, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let liveDecAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(pushFree, op: StdOp.globalAddr(liveDecAddr, label: GtLiveCountLabel))
	let liveDecOld = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(pushFree, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(liveDecOld, addrId: liveDecAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let liveDecOne = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(pushFree, op: StdOp.const(liveDecOne, value: 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let liveDecNew = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(pushFree, op: StdOp.binOp(liveDecNew, lhs: liveDecOld, rhs: liveDecOne, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(pushFree, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(liveDecAddr, offset: 0, valueId: liveDecNew, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(pushFree, op: StdOp.branch(loopHdr.id))

	// netpoll: the run queue is empty. Block the single M until something becomes ready — a parked TIMER fires or a
	// parked CHILD exits. `pc` = parked children, `earliest` = the nearest timer deadline (0 = none). When BOTH are
	// empty this is a deadlock (nothing ready, nothing pending) → done (the caller reads its result, matching the
	// original B1a bail). A pending child never bails: `haveProcs` always blocks on it.
	let pc = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(netpoll, op: StdOp.call(pc, callee: GtProcCountName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	let now = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(netpoll, op: StdOp.call(now, callee: GtNowNsName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	let earliest = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(netpoll, op: StdOp.call(earliest, callee: GtTimerEarliestName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	let noProcs = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(netpoll, op: StdOp.cmp(noProcs, lhs: pc, rhs: zeroConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(netpoll, op: StdOp.condBranch(noProcs, thenBlockId: timerOnly.id, elseBlockId: haveProcs.id))

	// timerOnly (pc == 0): the pre-subprocess path, byte-for-byte the same behaviour — no timer → deadlock done,
	// else decide.
	let noTimers = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(timerOnly, op: StdOp.cmp(noTimers, lhs: earliest, rhs: zeroConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(timerOnly, op: StdOp.condBranch(noTimers, thenBlockId: doneBlock.id, elseBlockId: timerDecide.id))

	// timerDecide: if the earliest deadline has passed → firenow, else → sleepwait.
	let isDue = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(timerDecide, op: StdOp.cmp(isDue, lhs: earliest, rhs: now, pred: StdCmpPred.le, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(timerDecide, op: StdOp.condBranch(isDue, thenBlockId: firenow.id, elseBlockId: sleepwait.id))

	// firenow: wake the due GTs (and, in the self-drive case, set our own status) then re-check the loop. SHARED by
	// the timer-only path and the process path's "a timer is already due" case — fire it without blocking on procs.
	let checkResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(firenow, op: StdOp.call(checkResult, callee: GtTimerCheckName, args: argsOf(now)))
	module.setTerminator(firenow, op: StdOp.branch(loopHdr.id))

	// sleepwait: pc == 0, earliest > now → plain Sleep on the timer delta, then re-poll (no GT is enqueued yet, so
	// re-entering netpoll cannot hit the deadlock bail — the timer is still in the store until `firenow` fires it).
	let sleepMs = emitCeilMs(module, block: sleepwait, ids: ids, earliest: earliest, now: now)
	module.appendOp(sleepwait, op: StdOp.osSleepMs(sleepMs))
	module.setTerminator(sleepwait, op: StdOp.branch(netpoll.id))

	// haveProcs (pc >= 1): block on the children. No timer bounds the wait → INFINITE; else bound it by the timer.
	let noTimersProcs = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(haveProcs, op: StdOp.cmp(noTimersProcs, lhs: earliest, rhs: zeroConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(haveProcs, op: StdOp.condBranch(noTimersProcs, thenBlockId: procInfinite.id, elseBlockId: procTimerDecide.id))

	// procInfinite: no timer → block on the children with INFINITE; a child exit is the only thing that can wake
	// us. After the wait `__gt_proc_check` has enqueued whatever exited, so re-check via loopHdr (NOT netpoll — a
	// just-woken GT sits in the run queue, and netpoll would read the now-empty stores as a false deadlock).
	let infiniteConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(procInfinite, op: StdOp.const(infiniteConst, value: WaitInfinite, valueType: StdType.i64))
	emitProcWait(module, block: procInfinite, ids: ids, pc: pc, timeoutMs: infiniteConst)
	module.setTerminator(procInfinite, op: StdOp.branch(loopHdr.id))

	// procTimerDecide: a timer exists. If it is ALREADY due, fire it first (firenow) without blocking; else block
	// on the children bounded by the timer delta.
	let procIsDue = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(procTimerDecide, op: StdOp.cmp(procIsDue, lhs: earliest, rhs: now, pred: StdCmpPred.le, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(procTimerDecide, op: StdOp.condBranch(procIsDue, thenBlockId: firenow.id, elseBlockId: procWaitBounded.id))

	// procWaitBounded: block on the children for at most the timer delta, then poll BOTH the children (in
	// `emitProcWait`) and — with a FRESH clock, since the wait consumed time — the timers, since either a child
	// exited OR the timer expired during the wait. Re-check via loopHdr, for the same reason procInfinite does.
	let boundedMs = emitCeilMs(module, block: procWaitBounded, ids: ids, earliest: earliest, now: now)
	emitProcWait(module, block: procWaitBounded, ids: ids, pc: pc, timeoutMs: boundedMs)
	let nowAfter = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(procWaitBounded, op: StdOp.call(nowAfter, callee: GtNowNsName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	let timerCheckResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(procWaitBounded, op: StdOp.call(timerCheckResult, callee: GtTimerCheckName, args: argsOf(nowAfter)))
	module.setTerminator(procWaitBounded, op: StdOp.branch(loopHdr.id))

	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.retVoid)
	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtDriveUntil'

// `__gt_await(promise) -> result` (when `throwing` is false) / `__gt_try_await(promise) -> (result, threw)`
// (when true) — a THIN wrapper over the shared `__gt_drive_until`: drive the scheduler until the promise's GT
// is `completed`, then extract the awaited scalar. Since P1.5 mid-body-yield, the drive loop is factored out
// (`__gt_drive_until`) and shared with a top-level `__gt_sleep`, so the two await drivers differ ONLY in the
// exit terminator (a plain `ret` vs the dual-register `errorReturn` for `try await`).
function buildGtAwaitDriver(module StdModule, name ByteArray, throwing bool)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(name, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let promise = 0 as ValueId

	// Drive the shared scheduler loop until the promise's own GT is `completed`, then extract its result. The
	// whole drive (dequeue, switch, netpoll, recycle) lives in `__gt_drive_until` — shared with a top-level
	// `__gt_sleep` — so this is a thin wrapper: call the drive, then read the awaited scalar (and, for
	// `try await`, the error flag) off the completed promise. A void drive call still mints an unread result id.
	let completedConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(completedConst, value: GtStatusCompleted, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let driveResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(driveResult, callee: GtDriveUntilName, args: args2(promise, second: completedConst)))

	// extract: result = promise.result. The exit `throwing` selects the terminator, the entire difference
	// between the two drivers — a non-throwing await returns it plainly (`ret`); a throwing `try await` also
	// loads `promise.threw` (the trampoline captured the thunk's R10 there) and exits through the dual-register
	// error ABI (`errorReturn`), which is what makes it a throwing function to the x64 backend. `__gt_await`
	// never reads `threw` — a throwing promise is only ever `try await`ed (E3057), so a non-throwing thunk's
	// garbage R10 never reaches a reader.
	let result = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(result, addrId: promise, offset: GtOffResult, valueType: StdType.i64))
	if throwing 'throwingExit'
		let threw = ids.mint()
		module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(threw, addrId: promise, offset: GtOffThrew, valueType: StdType.i64))
		module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.errorReturn(result, errorFlagId: threw))
	end 'throwingExit' else 'plainExit'
		module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.ret(result))
	end 'plainExit'

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtAwaitDriver'

// `__gt_sleep(ms)` — suspend the current GT for `ms` milliseconds, yielding to the scheduler. THE mid-body
// yield: compute the absolute deadline, park on the timer, then either self-drive (top-level GT0, which has no
// waiter above it) or switch back to the driver that ran us (a spawned GT). Lazily runs `__gt_init` first,
// because `main` may reach `sleep` without ever having spawned a green thread.
//
// ⭐ THE YIELD REUSES `__gt_context_switch` — no new hand-assembled primitive. A spawned GT switches to its
// `waiter` (the driver set it in `__gt_drive_until`'s `run` block) exactly as the trampoline's completion
// switch does; the only difference is that a parked GT is `waiting`, not `completed`, so the driver KEEPS its
// stack and switches back INTO it when its timer fires. A top-level GT0 has `waiter == 0`, so it cannot switch
// away — it self-drives the scheduler (`__gt_drive_until(self, ready)`) until its own timer fires, which
// degenerates to a plain `Sleep` when the run queue is empty (the sleep-basic case).
function buildGtSleep(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtSleepName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var selfDrive = module.addBlock(func, label: b"selfdrive")
	var parkSwitch = module.addBlock(func, label: b"parkswitch")
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let ms = 0 as ValueId

	// Lazy one-time scheduler init (a sleep-only `main` never spawned). A void call mints an unread result id.
	let initResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(initResult, callee: GtInitName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))

	// deadline = __gt_now_ns() + ms * 1e6.
	let now = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(now, callee: GtNowNsName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	let milliConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(milliConst, value: NanosPerMilli, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let msNs = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.binOp(msNs, lhs: ms, rhs: milliConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let deadline = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.binOp(deadline, lhs: now, rhs: msNs, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))

	// selfGt = __gt_current_gt; park it on the timer and mark it waiting.
	let curAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(curAddr, label: GtCurrentGtLabel))
	let selfGt = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(selfGt, addrId: curAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let addResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(addResult, callee: GtTimerAddName, args: args2(selfGt, second: deadline)))
	let waitingConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(waitingConst, value: GtStatusWaiting, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(selfGt, offset: GtOffStatus, valueId: waitingConst, valueType: StdType.i64))

	// waiter == 0 (top-level GT0) → self-drive; else (spawned GT) → switch back to the driver.
	let waiter = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(waiter, addrId: selfGt, offset: GtOffWaiter, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let zeroConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(zeroConst, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let waiterNull = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.cmp(waiterNull, lhs: waiter, rhs: zeroConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(waiterNull, thenBlockId: selfDrive.id, elseBlockId: parkSwitch.id))

	// selfDrive: drive the scheduler until our own timer fires (our status → ready), then return.
	let readyConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(selfDrive, op: StdOp.const(readyConst, value: GtStatusReady, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let driveResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(selfDrive, op: StdOp.call(driveResult, callee: GtDriveUntilName, args: args2(selfGt, second: readyConst)))
	module.setTerminator(selfDrive, op: StdOp.branch(doneBlock.id))

	// parkSwitch: mark parked (io_yielded=1), switch back to the driver; when the driver re-schedules us we
	// resume HERE — clear parked and return.
	let oneConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(parkSwitch, op: StdOp.const(oneConst, value: 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(parkSwitch, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(selfGt, offset: GtOffIoYielded, valueId: oneConst, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let switchResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(parkSwitch, op: StdOp.call(switchResult, callee: GtContextSwitchName, args: args2(selfGt, second: waiter)))
	let clearConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(parkSwitch, op: StdOp.const(clearConst, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(parkSwitch, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(selfGt, offset: GtOffIoYielded, valueId: clearConst, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(parkSwitch, op: StdOp.branch(doneBlock.id))

	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.retVoid)
	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtSleep'

// `__gt_proc_add(gt, handle)` — park `gt` on child `handle`: append `handle`/`gt` to the two parallel arrays,
// then mark `gt` waiting and record the handle in `gt.io_handle` (its durable home across the yield). An UNSORTED
// append (like `__gt_timer_add`), but — unlike the timer store — CAPACITY-GUARDED: parking a 65th child
// concurrently would write `procHandles[64]`/`procGts[64]` one slot PAST the 64-slot slab arrays (heap
// corruption), and then hand `WaitForMultipleObjects` a `pc > MAXIMUM_WAIT_OBJECTS` it refuses. So `count >=
// ProcStoreCapacity` aborts (`RuntimeAbort.processStoreOverflow`) rather than corrupt the heap — the seed's documented
// concurrency ceiling made a hard, safe bound (see `ProcStoreCapacity`).
function buildGtProcAdd(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtProcAddName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var overflow = module.addBlock(func, label: b"overflow")
	var doAdd = module.addBlock(func, label: b"doadd")
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let gt = 0 as ValueId
	let handle = 1 as ValueId

	// entry: load count; if it has reached the store's capacity → overflow (abort), else → doAdd.
	let countAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(countAddr, label: GtProcCountLabel))
	let count = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(count, addrId: countAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let capacityConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(capacityConst, value: ProcStoreCapacity, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let isFull = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.cmp(isFull, lhs: count, rhs: capacityConst, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(isFull, thenBlockId: overflow.id, elseBlockId: doAdd.id))

	// overflow: the store is full — abort rather than write past the 64-slot arrays.
	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: overflow, abort: RuntimeAbort.processStoreOverflow, ids: ids)

	// doAdd: append the entry to both parallel arrays, bump the count, and mark the GT waiting.
	let slotBytesConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doAdd, op: StdOp.const(slotBytesConst, value: ProcSlotBytes, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let entryOff = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doAdd, op: StdOp.binOp(entryOff, lhs: count, rhs: slotBytesConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, operandType: StdType.i64))
	// procHandles[count] = handle.
	let handlesBaseAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doAdd, op: StdOp.globalAddr(handlesBaseAddr, label: GtProcHandlesBaseLabel))
	let handlesBase = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doAdd, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(handlesBase, addrId: handlesBaseAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let handleSlot = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doAdd, op: StdOp.binOp(handleSlot, lhs: handlesBase, rhs: entryOff, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(doAdd, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(handleSlot, offset: 0, valueId: handle, valueType: StdType.i64))
	// procGts[count] = gt.
	let gtsBaseAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doAdd, op: StdOp.globalAddr(gtsBaseAddr, label: GtProcGtsBaseLabel))
	let gtsBase = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doAdd, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(gtsBase, addrId: gtsBaseAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let gtSlot = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doAdd, op: StdOp.binOp(gtSlot, lhs: gtsBase, rhs: entryOff, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(doAdd, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(gtSlot, offset: 0, valueId: gt, valueType: StdType.i64))
	// count++.
	let oneConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doAdd, op: StdOp.const(oneConst, value: 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let newCount = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doAdd, op: StdOp.binOp(newCount, lhs: count, rhs: oneConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(doAdd, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(countAddr, offset: 0, valueId: newCount, valueType: StdType.i64))
	// gt.status = waiting ; gt.io_handle = handle.
	let waitingConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doAdd, op: StdOp.const(waitingConst, value: GtStatusWaiting, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(doAdd, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(gt, offset: GtOffStatus, valueId: waitingConst, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(doAdd, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(gt, offset: GtOffIoHandle, valueId: handle, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(doAdd, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtProcAdd'

// `__gt_proc_count() -> count` — the live parked-child count (the netpoll's WaitForMultipleObjects `nCount`).
function buildGtProcCount(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtProcCountName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	let countAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(countAddr, label: GtProcCountLabel))
	let count = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(count, addrId: countAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.ret(count))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtProcCount'

// `__gt_proc_check()` — poll every parked child; wake the GTs whose child has EXITED. For each entry:
// `osWaitHandle(handle, 0) == WAIT_OBJECT_0` means the child exited → set gt.status=ready, re-enqueue it (UNLESS
// it is `__gt_current_gt`, a self-driving GT0 already on its own stack), and remove the entry (swap the last live
// entry into the hole across BOTH parallel arrays, count--, re-examine the same index). Mirrors
// `__gt_timer_check`'s fire/afterfire, with a zero-timeout wait poll in place of the deadline compare.
function buildGtProcCheck(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtProcCheckName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var loopHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"loophdr")
	var loopBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"loopbody")
	var advance = module.addBlock(func, label: b"advance")
	var fire = module.addBlock(func, label: b"fire")
	var doEnqueue = module.addBlock(func, label: b"enqueue")
	var afterFire = module.addBlock(func, label: b"afterfire")
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done")

	let countAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(countAddr, label: GtProcCountLabel))
	let count0 = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(count0, addrId: countAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let handlesBaseAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(handlesBaseAddr, label: GtProcHandlesBaseLabel))
	let handlesBase = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(handlesBase, addrId: handlesBaseAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let gtsBaseAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(gtsBaseAddr, label: GtProcGtsBaseLabel))
	let gtsBase = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(gtsBase, addrId: gtsBaseAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let zeroConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(zeroConst, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let slotBytesConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(slotBytesConst, value: ProcSlotBytes, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(loopHdr.id))

	// loopHdr(i, count): if i >= count → done(count), else → loopBody.
	let i = ids.mint()
	let count = ids.mint()
	loopHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(i, argType: StdType.i64))
	loopHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(count, argType: StdType.i64))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(loopHdr.id, argIds: args2(zeroConst, second: count0)))
	let atEnd = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(loopHdr, op: StdOp.cmp(atEnd, lhs: i, rhs: count, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(loopHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(atEnd, thenBlockId: doneBlock.id, elseBlockId: loopBody.id))

	// loopBody: h = procHandles[i]; poll it; if signaled (child exited) → fire, else → advance.
	let off = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(loopBody, op: StdOp.binOp(off, lhs: i, rhs: slotBytesConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let handleAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(loopBody, op: StdOp.binOp(handleAddr, lhs: handlesBase, rhs: off, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let handle = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(loopBody, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(handle, addrId: handleAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let pollTimeoutConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(loopBody, op: StdOp.const(pollTimeoutConst, value: WaitPollTimeout, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let waitResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(loopBody, op: StdOp.osWaitHandle(waitResult, handleId: handle, timeoutMsId: pollTimeoutConst))
	let signaledConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(loopBody, op: StdOp.const(signaledConst, value: WaitObject0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let isSignaled = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(loopBody, op: StdOp.cmp(isSignaled, lhs: waitResult, rhs: signaledConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(loopBody, op: StdOp.condBranch(isSignaled, thenBlockId: fire.id, elseBlockId: advance.id))

	// advance: not signaled → loopHdr(i+1, count).
	let oneAdv = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(advance, op: StdOp.const(oneAdv, value: 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let iAdv = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(advance, op: StdOp.binOp(iAdv, lhs: i, rhs: oneAdv, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(advance, op: StdOp.branch(loopHdr.id))
	advance.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(loopHdr.id, argIds: args2(iAdv, second: count)))

	// fire: gt = procGts[i]; gt.status = ready; skip enqueue when gt is the current (self-driving) GT.
	let fireOff = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(fire, op: StdOp.binOp(fireOff, lhs: i, rhs: slotBytesConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let gtAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(fire, op: StdOp.binOp(gtAddr, lhs: gtsBase, rhs: fireOff, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let gt = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(fire, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(gt, addrId: gtAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let readyConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(fire, op: StdOp.const(readyConst, value: GtStatusReady, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(fire, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(gt, offset: GtOffStatus, valueId: readyConst, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let curAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(fire, op: StdOp.globalAddr(curAddr, label: GtCurrentGtLabel))
	let curGt = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(fire, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(curGt, addrId: curAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let isCurrent = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(fire, op: StdOp.cmp(isCurrent, lhs: gt, rhs: curGt, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(fire, op: StdOp.condBranch(isCurrent, thenBlockId: afterFire.id, elseBlockId: doEnqueue.id))

	// doEnqueue: put the woken GT back on the run queue.
	let enqResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doEnqueue, op: StdOp.call(enqResult, callee: GtEnqueueName, args: argsOf(gt)))
	module.setTerminator(doEnqueue, op: StdOp.branch(afterFire.id))

	// afterFire: remove entry i — copy the last live entry over it in BOTH arrays, count--, re-examine i.
	let oneRem = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterFire, op: StdOp.const(oneRem, value: 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let newCount = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterFire, op: StdOp.binOp(newCount, lhs: count, rhs: oneRem, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let lastOff = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterFire, op: StdOp.binOp(lastOff, lhs: newCount, rhs: slotBytesConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let curOff = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterFire, op: StdOp.binOp(curOff, lhs: i, rhs: slotBytesConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, operandType: StdType.i64))
	// A self-copy when i == newCount is harmless. procHandles[i] = procHandles[last].
	let lastHandleAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterFire, op: StdOp.binOp(lastHandleAddr, lhs: handlesBase, rhs: lastOff, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let movedHandle = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterFire, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(movedHandle, addrId: lastHandleAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let holeHandleAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterFire, op: StdOp.binOp(holeHandleAddr, lhs: handlesBase, rhs: curOff, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(afterFire, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(holeHandleAddr, offset: 0, valueId: movedHandle, valueType: StdType.i64))
	// procGts[i] = procGts[last].
	let lastGtAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterFire, op: StdOp.binOp(lastGtAddr, lhs: gtsBase, rhs: lastOff, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let movedGt = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterFire, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(movedGt, addrId: lastGtAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let holeGtAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterFire, op: StdOp.binOp(holeGtAddr, lhs: gtsBase, rhs: curOff, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(afterFire, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(holeGtAddr, offset: 0, valueId: movedGt, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(afterFire, op: StdOp.branch(loopHdr.id))
	afterFire.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(loopHdr.id, argIds: args2(i, second: newCount)))

	// done(count): publish the surviving entry count.
	let doneCount = ids.mint()
	doneBlock.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(doneCount, argType: StdType.i64))
	loopHdr.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(doneBlock.id, argIds: argsOf(count)))
	let doneCountAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doneBlock, op: StdOp.globalAddr(doneCountAddr, label: GtProcCountLabel))
	module.appendOp(doneBlock, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(doneCountAddr, offset: 0, valueId: doneCount, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtProcCheck'

// `__gt_process_run(strPtr) -> code` — spawn the Windows child named by the command-line String, wait for it to
// exit (yielding the current GT while it runs), and return its scalar exit code. THE subprocess mid-body yield.
// The command line is BORROWED: `strPtr` is the 48-byte String record, whose `buffer`/`length` this reads exactly
// as `__print_string` does; ownership stays with the caller. Lazily runs `__gt_init` first (a top-level
// `runProcess` may reach here without ever having spawned a green thread).
function buildGtProcessRun(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtProcessRunName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var growCmBuf = module.addBlock(func, label: b"growcmbuf")
	var reuseCmBuf = module.addBlock(func, label: b"reusecmbuf")
	var haveCmBuf = module.addBlock(func, label: b"havecmbuf")
	var copyHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"copyhdr")
	var copyBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"copybody")
	var afterCopy = module.addBlock(func, label: b"aftercopy")
	var spawnFailed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"spawnfailed")
	var spawnOk = module.addBlock(func, label: b"spawnok")
	var selfDrive = module.addBlock(func, label: b"selfdrive")
	var parkSwitch = module.addBlock(func, label: b"parkswitch")
	var reap = module.addBlock(func, label: b"reap")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let strPtr = 0 as ValueId

	// Lazy one-time scheduler init. A void call mints an unread result id.
	let initResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(initResult, callee: GtInitName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))

	// cmdlinePtr = strPtr.buffer ; cmdlineLen = strPtr.length — borrowed, exactly as `__print_string` reads them.
	let cmdlinePtr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(cmdlinePtr, addrId: strPtr, offset: StringBufferOffset, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let cmdlineLen = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(cmdlineLen, addrId: strPtr, offset: StringLengthOffset, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let zeroConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(zeroConst, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))

	// P1.5-B1c #92: the STARTUPINFOA and PROCESS_INFORMATION scratch are the ONE-TIME reusable buffers `__gt_init`
	// allocated — load their bases rather than `__slab_alloc` fresh ones per call. `startup.cb` was set once in
	// init (CreateProcessA only reads it). PROCESS_INFORMATION is REUSED, so its `hProcess` must be RE-ZEROED here:
	// CreateProcessA leaves the struct UNTOUCHED on failure, so a stale non-zero handle from a prior successful
	// spawn would make the `hProcess == 0` failure sentinel below read a FAILED spawn as "spawned OK" and park on a
	// dead handle. Zeroing hProcess restores the fresh-slab invariant the sentinel relies on.
	let startupBaseAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(startupBaseAddr, label: GtStartupBaseLabel))
	let startupPtr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(startupPtr, addrId: startupBaseAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let procInfoBaseAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(procInfoBaseAddr, label: GtProcInfoBaseLabel))
	let procInfoPtr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(procInfoPtr, addrId: procInfoBaseAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(procInfoPtr, offset: ProcInfoHProcessOffset, valueId: zeroConst, valueType: StdType.i64))

	// The MUTABLE, NUL-terminated cmdline copy (CreateProcessA may write to lpCommandLine, and the .rdata String
	// blob is neither writable nor NUL-terminated) is the GROW-ON-DEMAND reusable buffer: reuse it when
	// `cmdlineLen + 1 <= cap`, else `__slab_alloc` a bigger one and update base+cap (the old one bump-"leaks", but
	// only when a strictly-longer command than any before appears — bounded, not per-call). The trailing byte is
	// the buffer's NUL: a freshly grown slab buffer is zeroed there; on REUSE the copy loop writes exactly
	// `cmdlineLen` bytes and the byte at `cmBuf[cmdlineLen]` still holds the prior (shorter-or-equal) command's NUL
	// or slab zero — so it is re-zeroed here to guarantee the terminator regardless of the previous tenant.
	let oneConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(oneConst, value: TrailingNulBytes, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let cmBufSize = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.binOp(cmBufSize, lhs: cmdlineLen, rhs: oneConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let cmBufBaseAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(cmBufBaseAddr, label: GtCmdlineBufBaseLabel))
	let cmBufCapAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(cmBufCapAddr, label: GtCmdlineBufCapLabel))
	let cmBufCap = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(cmBufCap, addrId: cmBufCapAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let needGrow = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.cmp(needGrow, lhs: cmBufSize, rhs: cmBufCap, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(needGrow, thenBlockId: growCmBuf.id, elseBlockId: reuseCmBuf.id))

	// growCmBuf: the buffer is too small (or first use, cap 0) — allocate `cmBufSize`, publish base+cap → haveCmBuf.
	let grownBuf = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(growCmBuf, op: StdOp.call(grownBuf, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(cmBufSize)))
	module.appendOp(growCmBuf, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(cmBufBaseAddr, offset: 0, valueId: grownBuf, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(growCmBuf, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(cmBufCapAddr, offset: 0, valueId: cmBufSize, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(growCmBuf, op: StdOp.branch(haveCmBuf.id))
	growCmBuf.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(haveCmBuf.id, argIds: argsOf(grownBuf)))

	// reuseCmBuf: the existing buffer is big enough — reuse its base → haveCmBuf.
	let reusedBuf = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(reuseCmBuf, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(reusedBuf, addrId: cmBufBaseAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(reuseCmBuf, op: StdOp.branch(haveCmBuf.id))
	reuseCmBuf.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(haveCmBuf.id, argIds: argsOf(reusedBuf)))

	// haveCmBuf(cmBuf): the reusable cmdline buffer (grown or reused) → re-zero its NUL terminator, then copy.
	let cmBuf = ids.mint()
	haveCmBuf.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(cmBuf, argType: StdType.i64))
	let nulAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(haveCmBuf, op: StdOp.binOp(nulAddr, lhs: cmBuf, rhs: cmdlineLen, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(haveCmBuf, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(nulAddr, offset: 0, valueId: zeroConst, valueType: StdType.u8))
	module.setTerminator(haveCmBuf, op: StdOp.branch(copyHdr.id))
	haveCmBuf.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(copyHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(zeroConst)))

	// copyHdr(i): the byte-copy loop guard. cmdlines are short, so a byte loop is well within budget.
	let i = ids.mint()
	copyHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(i, argType: StdType.i64))
	let atEnd = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(copyHdr, op: StdOp.cmp(atEnd, lhs: i, rhs: cmdlineLen, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(copyHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(atEnd, thenBlockId: afterCopy.id, elseBlockId: copyBody.id))

	// copyBody: cmBuf[i] = (u8) cmdlinePtr[i] ; loop to copyHdr(i+1). A u8 load/store — one command byte.
	let srcAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(copyBody, op: StdOp.binOp(srcAddr, lhs: cmdlinePtr, rhs: i, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let srcByte = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(copyBody, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(srcByte, addrId: srcAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.u8))
	let dstAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(copyBody, op: StdOp.binOp(dstAddr, lhs: cmBuf, rhs: i, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(copyBody, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(dstAddr, offset: 0, valueId: srcByte, valueType: StdType.u8))
	let oneStep = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(copyBody, op: StdOp.const(oneStep, value: 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let iNext = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(copyBody, op: StdOp.binOp(iNext, lhs: i, rhs: oneStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(copyBody, op: StdOp.branch(copyHdr.id))
	copyBody.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(copyHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(iNext)))

	// afterCopy: spawn the child. On FAILURE (`hProcess == 0` — CreateProcessA left the slab-zeroed
	// PROCESS_INFORMATION untouched) → `spawnFailed` (abort, never park on a null handle). On success → `spawnOk`.
	module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.osProcessSpawn(cmBuf, startupPtrId: startupPtr, procInfoPtrId: procInfoPtr))
	let hProcess = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(hProcess, addrId: procInfoPtr, offset: ProcInfoHProcessOffset, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let spawnZeroConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.const(spawnZeroConst, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let spawnFailedCmp = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.cmp(spawnFailedCmp, lhs: hProcess, rhs: spawnZeroConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(afterCopy, op: StdOp.condBranch(spawnFailedCmp, thenBlockId: spawnFailed.id, elseBlockId: spawnOk.id))

	// spawnFailed: the command names no runnable executable. Abort the whole process rather than park on a null
	// handle (see `RuntimeAbort.subprocessSpawnFailed`). This function RETURNS a value, so its dead terminator
	// carries one — which `emitRuntimeAbort` derives from `func.returnType`.
	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: spawnFailed, abort: RuntimeAbort.subprocessSpawnFailed, ids: ids)

	// spawnOk: close the (unneeded) thread handle, park the current GT on the process, and hand control to the
	// scheduler. Mirrors `__gt_sleep`'s park: a top-level GT0 (waiter == 0) SELF-DRIVES until its own
	// timer/process fires; a spawned GT switches back to the driver that ran it and resumes on re-schedule.
	let hThread = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(spawnOk, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(hThread, addrId: procInfoPtr, offset: ProcInfoHThreadOffset, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(spawnOk, op: StdOp.osCloseHandle(hThread))
	// selfGt = __gt_current_gt ; park it on the child (`__gt_proc_add` sets status=waiting, io_handle=hProcess).
	let curAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(spawnOk, op: StdOp.globalAddr(curAddr, label: GtCurrentGtLabel))
	let selfGt = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(spawnOk, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(selfGt, addrId: curAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let addResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(spawnOk, op: StdOp.call(addResult, callee: GtProcAddName, args: args2(selfGt, second: hProcess)))
	// waiter == 0 (top-level GT0) → self-drive; else (spawned GT) → switch back to the driver.
	let waiter = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(spawnOk, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(waiter, addrId: selfGt, offset: GtOffWaiter, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let zeroWaiterConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(spawnOk, op: StdOp.const(zeroWaiterConst, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let waiterNull = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(spawnOk, op: StdOp.cmp(waiterNull, lhs: waiter, rhs: zeroWaiterConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(spawnOk, op: StdOp.condBranch(waiterNull, thenBlockId: selfDrive.id, elseBlockId: parkSwitch.id))

	// selfDrive: drive the scheduler until our own process fires (our status → ready), then reap. The drive's
	// netpoll blocks the single M on the child's handle (and any timer delta) — a real wait, never a busy-spin.
	let readyConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(selfDrive, op: StdOp.const(readyConst, value: GtStatusReady, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let driveResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(selfDrive, op: StdOp.call(driveResult, callee: GtDriveUntilName, args: args2(selfGt, second: readyConst)))
	module.setTerminator(selfDrive, op: StdOp.branch(reap.id))

	// parkSwitch: mark parked (io_yielded=1), switch back to the driver; when it re-schedules us we resume HERE —
	// clear parked and reap. Reuses `__gt_context_switch`, exactly as `__gt_sleep`'s park does.
	let oneYieldConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(parkSwitch, op: StdOp.const(oneYieldConst, value: 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(parkSwitch, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(selfGt, offset: GtOffIoYielded, valueId: oneYieldConst, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let switchResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(parkSwitch, op: StdOp.call(switchResult, callee: GtContextSwitchName, args: args2(selfGt, second: waiter)))
	let clearYieldConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(parkSwitch, op: StdOp.const(clearYieldConst, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(parkSwitch, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(selfGt, offset: GtOffIoYielded, valueId: clearYieldConst, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(parkSwitch, op: StdOp.branch(reap.id))

	// reap: the child has exited. Reload hProcess from `selfGt.io_handle` (its durable home across the yield — the
	// value in a register cannot be assumed to survive an arbitrary number of intervening switches), then read the
	// exit code and close the handle. GetExitCodeProcess(hProcess, codePtr) ; code = codePtr[0] (i64 read).
	// ⭐ P1.5-B1c #92: codePtr is the ONE-TIME reusable exit-code slot (`__gt_exitcode_base`), not a per-call
	// `__slab_alloc`. THE PRE-ZERO IS WHY THE i64 READ IS CLEAN: GetExitCodeProcess writes only the low DWORD (4
	// bytes) of the 8-byte slot, so on a REUSED slot a prior call's low DWORD and any dirty high DWORD must be
	// cleared first for an i64 load to yield exactly this child's code (a full-word load is the only wide memory
	// form — a u32 read would need an `as` cast the runtime builder cannot spell). The fresh-slab zeroing that used
	// to satisfy this is now an explicit `store 0` before the call, since the slot is reused across calls.
	let reapHandle = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(reap, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(reapHandle, addrId: selfGt, offset: GtOffIoHandle, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let codeBaseAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(reap, op: StdOp.globalAddr(codeBaseAddr, label: GtExitCodeBaseLabel))
	let codePtr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(reap, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(codePtr, addrId: codeBaseAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let codeZeroConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(reap, op: StdOp.const(codeZeroConst, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(reap, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(codePtr, offset: 0, valueId: codeZeroConst, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(reap, op: StdOp.osProcessExitCode(reapHandle, codePtrId: codePtr))
	let code = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(reap, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(code, addrId: codePtr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(reap, op: StdOp.osCloseHandle(reapHandle))
	module.setTerminator(reap, op: StdOp.ret(code))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtProcessRun'
